QB.add(11,[{"id":11001,"t":"Intro to the Muscular System","s":"Which statement summarises how muscles produce movement?","o":["Nerve stimulation makes two proteins slide, powered by ATP","Nerve stimulation makes the filaments themselves shorten, powered by ATP","Nerve stimulation makes two proteins slide, powered directly by glucose","Hormonal stimulation makes two proteins slide, powered by ATP","Nerve stimulation makes muscles push bones apart, powered by ATP"],"e":"Muscles move parts of the body as a result of <b>electrical stimulation from nerves</b>. Contraction involves <b>two proteins (actin and myosin) sliding over each other</b>, needing energy as <b>ATP</b> – a coenzyme that helps energy transfer.","w":["Filaments do not shorten; they slide.","ATP is the energy source.","Electrical stimulation from nerves.","Muscles pull."],"k":"Nerve stimulus → actin/myosin slide → needs ATP.","rn":[["","What makes muscles move parts of the body?","What makes muscles move parts of the body?","Electrical stimulation from nerves"]]},{"id":11002,"t":"Components of Muscle Tissue","f":"mu10","s":"From outside to inside, which order of connective-tissue wrappings is correct?","o":["Epimysium → perimysium → endomysium","Endomysium → perimysium → epimysium","Perimysium → epimysium → endomysium","Epimysium → endomysium → perimysium","Epimysium → fascicle → endomysium"],"e":"<b>Epimysium</b> surrounds the entire muscle; <b>perimysium</b> each <b>fascicle</b> (bundle); <b>endomysium</b> each individual <b>fibre</b>. A fascicle is a bundle of fibres, not a wrapping. (Epi = upon, peri = around, endo = within.)","w":["Reversed.","Epimysium is outermost.","Perimysium is middle.","A fascicle is a bundle, not a wrapping."],"k":"Epi → peri → endo.","rn":[["","What are the 3 connective tissue layers of a muscle?","What are the 3 connective tissue layers of a muscle?","Epimysium; Perimysium; Endomysium; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":11003,"t":"Components of Muscle Tissue","s":"What is a bundle of muscle fibres wrapped by perimysium called?","e":"A fascicle is a bundle of muscle fibres wrapped by perimysium.","k":"Fascicle.","a":["fascicle","fasciculus","muscle fascicle","fascicles"],"rn":[["","Muscle Fascicle","Muscle Fascicle","A bundle of muscle fibres"]]},{"id":11004,"t":"Components of Muscle Tissue","f":"mu11","s":"How do blood vessels and nerves reach individual muscle fibres?","o":["Within the connective-tissue layers","Directly through the sarcomeres","Only through the tendon","Within the sarcoplasmic reticulum","Along the T-tubules from the surface"],"e":"A second view of muscle structure highlighted that <b>blood vessels and nerves travel within the connective-tissue layers</b> to reach every fibre.","w":["Not stated.","They reach every fibre.","Not stated.","T-tubules are part of the fibre."],"k":"Vessels/nerves run in the wrappings.","rn":[["","What are the 3 connective tissue layers of a muscle?","What are the 3 connective tissue layers of a muscle?","Epimysium; Perimysium; Endomysium; MNEMONIC"],["","Tendon","Tendon","Thick fibrous connective tissue; Attaches muscle to bone"]]},{"id":11005,"t":"Components of Muscle Tissue","s":"Which pairing of general cell term and muscle-cell term is correct?","o":["ER – sarcoplasmic reticulum","Cell membrane – sarcoplasm","Cytoplasm – sarcolemma","Functional contractile unit – sarcolemma","Cytoplasm – sarcomere"],"e":"“Sarco” (Greek <i>sarx</i> = flesh): cell membrane → <b>sarcolemma</b>; cytoplasm → <b>sarcoplasm</b>; endoplasmic reticulum → <b>sarcoplasmic reticulum</b>; functional contractile unit → <b>sarcomere</b>.","w":["Sarcolemma.","Sarcoplasm.","Sarcomere.","Sarcoplasm."],"k":"Sarco- terminology.","rn":[["","Endoplasmic Reticulum (in muscle)","Endoplasmic Reticulum (in muscle)","Sarcoplasmic Reticulum"],["","Image card: What is hidden under the ?","What is hidden under the ?","Sarcoplasmic reticulum"]]},{"id":11006,"t":"Intro to the Muscular System","s":"What is a myocyte, and what enables it to generate force?","o":["A muscle cell; actin–myosin interaction","A muscle cell; acetylcholine release","A nerve cell; actin–myosin interaction","A muscle cell; collagen fibre contraction","A satellite cell; actin–myosin interaction"],"e":"A <b>myocyte</b> is a muscle cell – a <b>contractile cell</b> adapted to generate motile forces through the <b>interaction of actin and myosin</b>.","w":["ACh is from the nerve.","A muscle cell.","Not collagen.","A muscle cell."],"k":"Myocyte = contractile muscle cell.","rn":[["","What is a myocyte?","What is a myocyte?","Contractile muscle cell; Adapted to generate motile forces"],["","What 2 proteins interact in muscle cells?","What 2 proteins interact in muscle cells?","Actin; Myosin"]]},{"id":11007,"t":"Types of Muscle","f":"mu15","s":"A biopsy shows long striated fibres with many nuclei pushed to the periphery. Which muscle type is this?","o":["Skeletal","Cardiac","Smooth","Mixed cardiac/smooth","Tendon"],"e":"<b>Skeletal muscle</b>: striated, <b>multinucleated</b> with <b>peripheral nuclei</b>, because each fibre forms from the fusion of many hundreds of <b>myoblasts</b> and the packed myofibrils push nuclei to the edge.","w":["Central nucleus, intercalated discs.","Non-striated, central nucleus.","Not a type.","Collagen, few flat nuclei."],"k":"Striated + peripheral nuclei = skeletal.","rn":[["","Where are the nuclei in skeletal muscle cells?","Where are the nuclei in skeletal muscle cells?","Peripheral; At the cell edge"],["","How many nuclei does a mature skeletal muscle cell have?","How many nuclei does a mature skeletal muscle cell have?","Many; Multinucleated"]]},{"id":11008,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"Why are skeletal muscle fibres multinucleated?","o":["Each forms from fusion of hundreds of myoblasts","Each divides its nucleus repeatedly without dividing","Each gains nuclei from satellite cells only","Each forms by fusion through intercalated discs","Each forms from one myoblast with many nuclei"],"e":"Each skeletal muscle cell forms from the <b>fusion of many hundreds of myoblasts</b>, so mature cells are <b>multinucleated</b> with <b>peripheral</b> nuclei.","w":["Not stated.","Satellite cells add nuclei later; formation is by myoblast fusion.","Cardiac.","Hundreds fuse."],"k":"Myoblast fusion → multinucleated fibres.","rn":[["","How does a skeletal muscle cell form?","How does a skeletal muscle cell form?","Fusion of hundreds; Of precursor cells (myoblasts)"]]},{"id":11009,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"What determines the force of contraction of a skeletal muscle?","o":["Its cross-sectional area","Its length only","The number of intercalated discs","The number of Purkinje fibres","Its colour"],"e":"Skeletal muscle is capable of powerful contractions – <b>force is proportional to cross-sectional area</b>. This is also why pennate muscles are stronger.","w":["Not stated.","Cardiac feature.","Cardiac conduction.","Not stated."],"k":"Force ∝ cross-sectional area.","rn":[["","What is the force of skeletal muscle proportional to?","What is the force of skeletal muscle proportional to?","Cross-sectional area"]]},{"id":11010,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"Which nerves supply skeletal muscle?","o":["Somatic and branchial motor nerves","Somatic motor nerves only","Autonomic secretomotor nerves","Visceral motor nerves (vagus and sympathetic)","Branchial motor nerves only"],"e":"Skeletal muscle is innervated by <b>somatic</b> and <b>branchial motor</b> nerves. Branchial motor fibres in CN V, VII, IX, X and XI supply muscles derived from the branchial (pharyngeal) arches, e.g. mastication and facial expression.","w":["Branchial too.","Smooth muscle.","Cardiac muscle.","Somatic too."],"k":"Skeletal: somatic + branchial motor.","rn":[["","What nerves innervate Skeletal Muscle?","What nerves innervate Skeletal Muscle?","Somatic motor nerves; Branchial motor nerves"]]},{"id":11011,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"Which muscles are supplied by branchial motor fibres in cranial nerves?","o":["Mastication, facial expression, palate, pharynx, larynx, trapezius, SCM","Mastication, facial expression, palate, pharynx, larynx, intercostals","Heart, great vessels and pharynx","Mastication, facial expression, tongue and diaphragm","Pharynx, larynx and the smooth muscle of the gut"],"e":"Branchial motor = special visceral efferent fibres in <b>CN V, VII, IX, X and XI</b>, supplying muscles of <b>mastication, facial expression, the palate, pharynx, larynx, trapezius and sternocleidomastoid</b> (derived from the branchial/pharyngeal arches).","w":["Intercostals are not branchial.","Cardiac.","Not listed.","Gut is smooth muscle."],"k":"Branchial motor targets.","rn":[["","Which muscles do branchial motor nerves supply?","Which muscles do branchial motor nerves supply?","Mastication; facial expression; Palate, pharynx, larynx; Trapezius and sternocleidomastoid"]]},{"id":11012,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"In this transverse section, fibres are polygonal with nuclei (1) pushed to the periphery and thin septa (2) between them. Name the muscle type.","e":"Skeletal muscle in transverse section: polygonal fibres with peripheral nuclei and fibrocollagenous septa. Fibres are multinucleated because hundreds of myoblasts fuse.","k":"Peripheral nuclei, polygonal = skeletal.","img":{"src":"11/skeletalTS","cap":"Muscle tissue, transverse section, H&amp;E."},"a":["skeletal","skeletal muscle","striated skeletal muscle"],"rn":[["","Image card: What type of muscle is being shown? How can you tell?","What type of muscle is being shown? How can you tell?","Skeletal muscle (transverse); Polygonal fibres, peripheral nuclei; Fibrocollagenous septa (S)"]]},{"id":11013,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"Which statement about cardiac muscle contraction is correct?","o":["Less powerful than skeletal, but fatigue-resistant","More powerful than skeletal, and fatigues quickly","Less powerful than skeletal, and fatigues quickly","Slow and sustained, like smooth muscle","More powerful than skeletal, and fatigue-resistant"],"e":"Cardiac contractions are <b>less powerful than skeletal</b> but <b>resistant to fatigue</b>. Smooth muscle gives slow, sustained contractions.","w":["Less powerful.","Fatigue-resistant.","That is smooth.","Less powerful."],"k":"Cardiac: less powerful, fatigue-resistant.","rn":[["","Cardiac vs skeletal: power and fatigue","Cardiac vs skeletal: power and fatigue","Cardiac: less powerful; Cardiac: resistant to fatigue"]]},{"id":11014,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"Which nerves supply cardiac muscle?","o":["Visceral motor: vagus (CN X) and sympathetic trunk","Somatic motor: vagus (CN X) and sympathetic trunk","Branchial motor: vagus (CN X) and facial nerve","Autonomic secretomotor only","Visceral motor: phrenic nerve and sympathetic trunk"],"e":"Cardiac muscle is innervated by <b>visceral motor nerves</b>: <b>parasympathetic (vagus, CN X)</b> and the <b>sympathetic trunk</b> (from lower cervical and upper thoracic ganglia).","w":["Visceral.","Branchial is skeletal.","Smooth.","Not phrenic."],"k":"Cardiac: vagus + sympathetic trunk.","rn":[["","What nerves innervate Cardiac Muscle?","What nerves innervate Cardiac Muscle?","Visceral motor nerves; Parasympathetic (vagus); Sympathetic trunk"]]},{"id":11015,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"Name the conducting fibres that spread the signal rapidly through the ventricles.","e":"Purkinje fibres are a conducting system for rapid signal spread to the ventricles.","k":"Purkinje fibres.","a":["purkinje fibres","purkinje fibers","purkinje"],"rn":[["","Purkinje fibres","Purkinje fibres","Specialised conducting system; Rapid signals to ventricles"]]},{"id":11016,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"This tissue has spindle-shaped cells with cigar-shaped central nuclei and no striations. Name the muscle type.","e":"Smooth muscle: non-striated, spindle-shaped cells with central elongated nuclei; found in the gut wall and tunica media of vessels; involuntary, supplied by autonomic nerves.","k":"Smooth muscle.","img":{"src":"11/smooth","cap":"Muscle tissue, longitudinal section, H&amp;E."},"a":["smooth","smooth muscle","visceral muscle"],"rn":[["","Image card: What type of muscle is this? How can you tell?","What type of muscle is this? How can you tell?","Smooth muscle; No stripes; Spindle-shaped fibres"],["","Where is smooth muscle found in vessels and skin?","Where is smooth muscle found in vessels and skin?","Blood vessel walls (tunica media); Hair follicles"],["","What are the 3 types of muscle?","What are the 3 types of muscle?","Skeletal; Cardiac; Smooth"]]},{"id":11017,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"Which nerves supply smooth muscle?","o":["Autonomic secretomotor nerves","Somatic motor nerves","Branchial motor nerves","Visceral motor nerves to the heart","Musculocutaneous nerve"],"e":"Smooth muscle is innervated by <b>autonomic secretomotor</b> nerves, controlling involuntary processes. Somatic nerves supply voluntary skeletal muscle.","w":["Skeletal.","Skeletal.","Cardiac.","Biceps."],"k":"Smooth: autonomic secretomotor.","rn":[["","What nerves innervate Smooth Muscle?","What nerves innervate Smooth Muscle?","Autonomic secretomotor nerves"]]},{"id":11018,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"Which pairing of muscle type and control/nuclei is correct?","o":["Cardiac – involuntary, single central nucleus","Skeletal – involuntary, single central nucleus","Smooth – voluntary, peripheral nuclei","Cardiac – voluntary, many peripheral nuclei","Skeletal – voluntary, single central nucleus"],"e":"Skeletal: <b>voluntary</b>, striated, many peripheral nuclei. <b>Cardiac</b>: <b>involuntary</b>, striated, <b>single central</b> nucleus. Smooth: involuntary, non-striated, single central elongated nucleus.","w":["Skeletal is voluntary, peripheral.","Smooth is involuntary, central.","Cardiac is involuntary.","Skeletal nuclei are peripheral."],"k":"Muscle type summary table.","rn":[["","How do you tell cardiac from skeletal muscle on histology?","How do you tell cardiac from skeletal muscle on histology?","Central nucleus; Intercalated discs; Branching"],["","Cardiac Muscle: Control, Appearance, Association?","Cardiac Muscle: Control, Appearance, Association?","Involuntary; Striated; Heart (visceral)"]]},{"id":11019,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"This transverse section shows irregular, lobulated cells with central nuclei (1) and many small blood vessels (2) between them. Which muscle type, and how is it supplied?","o":["Cardiac; involuntary, autonomic nerves, rich capillary supply","Skeletal; voluntary, somatic nerves, few capillaries","Smooth; voluntary, somatic nerves","Cardiac; voluntary, somatic nerves","Skeletal; involuntary, autonomic nerves"],"e":"Cardiac muscle in transverse section: elliptical/lobulated cells with central nuclei and a rich capillary supply in the septa. It is involuntary, supplied by autonomic nerves, and contracts rhythmically (Purkinje fibres spread the signal).","w":["Skeletal fibres have peripheral nuclei and polygonal outlines.","Smooth muscle is involuntary.","Cardiac muscle is involuntary.","Skeletal muscle is voluntary."],"k":"Central nuclei + capillaries = cardiac.","img":{"src":"11/cardiacTS","cap":"Muscle tissue, transverse section, H&amp;E."},"rn":[["","Cardiac muscle in transverse section: what lies between cells?","Cardiac muscle in transverse section: what lies between cells?","Septa with small vessels; Rich capillary supply"],["","What do autonomic nerves supply?","What do autonomic nerves supply?","Involuntary physiological processes"]]},{"id":11020,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"What gives skeletal muscle its cross-striations?","o":["Alternating zones of thick (myosin) and thin (actin) filaments","Alternating zones of collagen and elastin fibres","Intercalated discs repeating along the fibre","Peripheral nuclei repeating along the fibre","Alternating zones of sarcoplasmic reticulum and T-tubules"],"e":"Cross-striations come from stacks of <b>myofibrils</b> made of alternating, overlapping zones of <b>thick (dark-stained) myosin</b> and <b>thin (light-stained) actin</b> filaments.","w":["Not collagen.","Cardiac.","Not the cause.","Not the cause."],"k":"Striations = thick/thin filament zones.","rn":[["","Which filaments make the dark and light zones?","Which filaments make the dark and light zones?","Dark: thick myosin; Light: thin actin"]]},{"id":11021,"t":"Components of Muscle Tissue","s":"Name the repeating contractile unit of a myofibril, between two Z lines.","e":"The sarcomere (Z line to Z line) is the contractile unit; in contraction the I band and H zone shorten while the A band stays the same.","k":"Sarcomere.","a":["sarcomere","sarcomeres"],"rn":[["","Functional Contractile Unit (of muscle)","Functional Contractile Unit (of muscle)","Sarcomere"],["","Image card: What is hidden under the ?","What is hidden under the ?","Sarcomere"]]},{"id":11022,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","s":"Which statement about the A band is correct?","o":["The dark myosin band, with overlap; length unchanged","The dark myosin band, with overlap; shortens","The light actin band, without overlap; unchanged","The light actin band, without overlap; narrows","The pale centre of the A band; disappears"],"e":"The <b>A band</b> (dark, <b>anisotropic</b>) is the length of the <b>thick (myosin)</b> filaments, including where thin filaments overlap; it <b>stays the same</b> during contraction.","w":["Stays the same.","That is the I band.","That narrows.","That is the H zone."],"k":"A band = thick filament length; unchanged.","rn":[["","A Band","A Band","Dark band; Anisotropic; Thick filaments (including overlap)"]]},{"id":11023,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","s":"What is the I band, and what happens to it during contraction?","o":["Light band of non-overlapping actin; it narrows","Light band of non-overlapping actin; stays the same","Dark band of myosin; it narrows","Dark band of myosin; stays the same","Pale centre of myosin only; it widens"],"e":"The <b>I band</b> (light, <b>isotropic</b>) is the zone of <b>thin (actin)</b> filaments that do <b>not</b> overlap thick filaments; it <b>narrows</b> as filaments slide.","w":["It narrows.","That is light.","That is the A band.","H zone."],"k":"I band: thin only; narrows.","rn":[["","I Band","I Band","Light band; Isotropic; Thin filaments only (no overlap)"],["","Which filaments make the dark and light zones?","Which filaments make the dark and light zones?","Dark: thick myosin; Light: thin actin"]]},{"id":11024,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","s":"Where are the H zone and M line located?","o":["H zone: pale centre of the A band; M line: centre of the H zone","H zone: centre of the I band; M line: sarcomere boundary","H zone: pale centre of the A band; M line: sarcomere boundary","H zone: actin-only region; M line: centre of the H zone","H zone: centre of the I band; M line: centre of the A band"],"e":"<b>H zone</b>: pale centre of the A band where <b>only thick filaments</b> are; <b>M line</b>: runs down the <b>centre of the H band</b>. The <b>Z line</b> lies in the centre of the I band and marks sarcomere ends.","w":["Z line is in the I band.","Z line is the boundary.","Only thick filaments.","Z is in the I band."],"k":"H zone and M line are central.","rn":[["","H Zone","H Zone","Pale area; Centre of the A band; No thin filament overlap"],["","M line","M line","Runs down the centre; Of the H band"],["","Z Line","Z Line","Dark line; Centre of the I band"]]},{"id":11025,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","s":"During contraction, which structures shorten and which stay the same?","o":["Sarcomere, I band, H zone shorten; A band does not","A band and filaments shorten; I band and H zone do not","Sarcomere and A band shorten; I band and H zone do not","Sarcomere, I band and A band shorten; the filaments themselves do not","Nothing changes; only the Z lines move apart"],"e":"“HI, I shrink; A stays the same”: the <b>sarcomere</b>, <b>I band</b> and <b>H zone</b> shorten (H may disappear); the <b>A band</b> and the <b>filament lengths</b> do not change – they slide. Z lines move closer.","w":["Swapped.","A stays.","The A band stays the same.","Z lines come closer."],"k":"Sliding filament changes.","rn":[["","Which bands change during contraction?","Which bands change during contraction?","H zone, I band shorten; A band stays same; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":11026,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","f":"mu26","s":"According to the sliding filament model, what happens during contraction?","o":["Thin filaments slide over thick towards the centre","Thick filaments slide over thin towards the Z lines","Thin filaments shorten and pull the Z lines in","Thick filaments shrink, increasing overlap","Thin filaments slide away from the centre"],"e":"During contraction <b>thin filaments slide over thick filaments</b>, increasing overlap; the light I bands narrow while the dark A bands stay the same. This is <b>reversed on relaxation</b>.","w":["Thin slide over thick.","No shortening.","No shrinking.","Towards."],"k":"Actin slides over myosin.","rn":[["","How does muscle contraction happen?","How does muscle contraction happen?","Filaments slide past each other; Thin over thick"]]},{"id":11027,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","s":"By about how much does a typical muscle fibre shorten during contraction?","o":["About one third","About one tenth","About one half","About two thirds","About one quarter"],"e":"A typical fibre <b>shortens by about 1/3</b> of its length.","w":["1/3.","1/3.","1/3.","1/3."],"k":"Fibre shortens ~⅓.","rn":[["","How much does a typical muscle fibre shorten by?","How much does a typical muscle fibre shorten by?","1/3"]]},{"id":11028,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","s":"How many thin filaments surround each thick filament in a sarcomere?","o":["Six","Two","Four","Eight","Twelve"],"e":"Within each sarcomere, <b>each thick filament is surrounded by six thin filaments</b>.","w":["Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Six thin around each thick.","rn":[["","How many thin filaments surround each thick filament?","How many thin filaments surround each thick filament?","Six"]]},{"id":11029,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","s":"What keeps actin and myosin precisely aligned, and how can it be seen?","o":["Accessory proteins, seen only with immunohistochemistry","Accessory proteins, seen with routine H&E staining","Intercalated discs, seen with H&E staining","Satellite cells, seen with light microscopy","T-tubules, seen with polarised light"],"e":"Function depends on <b>precise alignment</b> of actin and myosin, maintained by <b>accessory proteins</b> holding components in register; these can only be seen with <b>immunohistochemical</b> techniques.","w":["Immunohistochemistry only.","Cardiac.","Not alignment.","Not stated."],"k":"Accessory proteins keep filaments aligned.","rn":[["","What holds actin and myosin in precise alignment?","What holds actin and myosin in precise alignment?","Accessory proteins"]]},{"id":11030,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","s":"Why are A bands called “anisotropic” and I bands “isotropic”?","o":["A bands are birefringent in polarised light; I bands are not","I bands are birefringent in polarised light; A bands are not","A bands contain nuclei; I bands do not","A bands contain actin only; I bands myosin only","A and I bands are both birefringent"],"e":"<b>A bands</b> = dark, <b>anisotropic</b> (birefringent in polarised light). <b>I bands</b> = light, <b>isotropic</b> (no interference with polarised light).","w":["Swapped.","Not stated.","Wrong.","I bands are isotropic."],"k":"A = anisotropic; I = isotropic.","rn":[["","What does A band \"anisotropic\" mean?","What does A band \"anisotropic\" mean?","Birefringent in polarised light"]]},{"id":11031,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","s":"What are the four essential parameters to describe any muscle?","o":["Origin, insertion, action, innervation","Length, width, colour, weight","Agonist, antagonist, synergist, fixator","Epimysium, perimysium, endomysium, fascicle","A band, I band, H zone, Z line"],"e":"The four parameters: <b>origin</b> (proximal, usually stationary), <b>insertion</b> (distal, usually mobile), <b>action</b>, <b>innervation</b>.","w":["Not stated.","Functional roles.","Wrappings.","Sarcomere zones."],"k":"Origin, insertion, action, innervation.","rn":[["","What are the 4 essential parameters of a muscle?","What are the 4 essential parameters of a muscle?","Origin; Insertion; Action; Innervation; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":11032,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","f":"mu28","s":"Which description of biceps brachii is correct?","o":["Supraglenoid + coracoid → radial tuberosity; flexes; musculocutaneous","Supraglenoid tubercle + coracoid → radial tuberosity; extends; radial nerve","Coracoid only → radial tuberosity; flexes; musculocutaneous","Radial tuberosity → supraglenoid tubercle + coracoid; flexes; median","Supraglenoid tubercle + coracoid → ulna; pronates; musculocutaneous"],"e":"<b>Biceps brachii</b>: <b>2 heads</b> – supraglenoid tubercle (long) and coracoid process (short); inserts on the <b>bicipital (radial) tuberosity</b>; <b>flexes the forearm at the elbow</b> and <b>the arm at the shoulder</b>, aids <b>supination</b>; <b>musculocutaneous nerve</b>.","w":["Flexes; musculocutaneous.","Two heads.","Reversed; musculocutaneous.","Radial tuberosity; aids supination."],"k":"Biceps brachii worked example.","rn":[["","What is the Origin of Biceps Brachii?","What is the Origin of Biceps Brachii?","2 heads; Supraglenoid tubercle; Coracoid process"],["","What is the Insertion of Biceps Brachii?","What is the Insertion of Biceps Brachii?","Bicipital (radial) tuberosity; On the radius"]]},{"id":11033,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","s":"What distinguishes an origin from an insertion?","o":["Origin: proximal, usually still; insertion: distal, usually moves","Origin: distal, usually moves; insertion: proximal, usually still","Origin: the larger tendon; insertion: the smaller","Origin: proximal, usually moves; insertion: distal, usually still","Origin and insertion are interchangeable terms"],"e":"<b>Origin</b>: <b>proximal</b>, usually <b>stationary</b>. <b>Insertion</b>: <b>distal</b>, usually <b>mobile</b>.","w":["Swapped.","Not stated.","Swapped.","Different."],"k":"Origin proximal/stationary; insertion distal/mobile.","rn":[["","Origin","Origin","Proximal attachment; Usually the stationary end"],["","Insertion","Insertion","Distal attachment; Usually the mobile end"]]},{"id":11034,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","f":"mu29","s":"Which pairing of attachment type and description is correct?","o":["Aponeurosis – broad sheet anchoring muscle to bone","Aponeurosis – seam between two muscle sheets","Raphe – cord of dense tissue to bone","Tendon – broad sheet anchoring to bone","Raphe – the Achilles tendon"],"e":"<b>Tendon</b>: cord of dense connective tissue to bone (Achilles, fingers). <b>Aponeurosis</b>: <b>broad sheet</b> anchoring to bone (epicranial, abdominal, plantar). <b>Raphe</b>: broad anchorage to fascia where muscle sheets meet – a “seam” (e.g. mylohyoid, pharyngeal, anococcygeal).","w":["Raphe.","Tendon.","Aponeurosis.","Tendon."],"k":"Tendon, aponeurosis, raphe.","rn":[["","Aponeurosis","Aponeurosis","Thin sheet of connective tissue; Broad anchorage to bone"]]},{"id":11035,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","s":"A seam formed by the fused ends of two embryologically separate muscle sheets (e.g. the mylohyoid) is called what?","e":"Raphe (Greek “seam”): the fused ends of two sheets, e.g. mylohyoid, pterygomandibular, anococcygeal and pharyngeal raphes.","k":"Raphe.","a":["raphe","raphae"],"rn":[["","What is a raphe commonly?","What is a raphe commonly?","Fused ends; Of two flat muscle sheets"],["","Give 3 examples of raphes (face and neck)","Give 3 examples of raphes (face and neck)","Lateral palpebral raphe; Mylohyoid raphe; Pharyngeal raphe; pterygomandibular raphe"],["","Raphe","Raphe","A \"seam\"; Anchorage to fascia sheets; Between muscles"]]},{"id":11036,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","f":"mu30","s":"Which histological features describe a tendon?","o":["Packed longitudinal collagen with flat inactive fibrocyte nuclei","Packed longitudinal collagen with peripheral muscle nuclei","Striated fibres with peripheral nuclei","Spindle cells with central elongated nuclei","Branched cells joined by intercalated discs"],"e":"Tendon histology: <b>tightly packed, longitudinally running collagen fibres</b> with <b>elongated flattened nuclei of inactive fibrocytes</b> – strong in tension. The largest tendon is the <b>Achilles</b> (calf to calcaneum).","w":["Fibrocytes.","Skeletal.","Smooth.","Cardiac."],"k":"Tendon: parallel collagen, flat fibrocytes.","rn":[["","What does a tendon look like on histology?","What does a tendon look like on histology?","Tightly packed collagen fibres; Running longitudinally; Elongated flattened nuclei"]]},{"id":11037,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","f":"mu31","s":"What is the function of a tendon sheath, and where is one found?","o":["It protects from friction over bone, e.g. long finger flexors","It attaches the muscle to bone, e.g. the Achilles tendon","It protects from friction over bone, e.g. the epicranial aponeurosis","It produces new fibres after injury, e.g. in the biceps","It conducts nerve impulses to the tendon"],"e":"A <b>tendon sheath</b> <b>protects the tendon from repeated friction</b> where tendons <b>traverse bony surfaces</b>, e.g. the <b>long flexor tendons to the fingers</b>. Labels: T = tendon, E = epitendineum, S = sheath, TSS = fluid-filled sheath space.","w":["That is the tendon.","Not a sheath.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Tendon sheath reduces friction.","rn":[["","Where are tendon sheaths found?","Where are tendon sheaths found?","Where tendons cross bony surfaces; Long flexor tendons to fingers"],["","What is the main job of a tendon sheath?","What is the main job of a tendon sheath?","Protect tendon from; Repeated friction"],["","Image card: What are T, E, S and TSS?","What are T, E, S and TSS?","T: long flexor tendon; E: epitendineum, S: sheath; TSS: fluid-filled sheath space"]]},{"id":11038,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","s":"What fills the tendon sheath space, and what produces it?","o":["Lubricating fluid from flat, synovium-like lining cells","Lubricating fluid from satellite cells in the tendon","Blood from the nutrient artery of the bone","Lymph draining from the nearby lacteals","Synovial fluid from the nearest joint cavity"],"e":"The <b>tendon sheath space</b> contains <b>thin lubricating fluid</b> secreted by <b>flat lining cells</b>, which are like those forming the <b>intima of synovium</b>.","w":["Not satellite cells.","Not blood.","Not lymph.","Secreted by its own lining."],"k":"Sheath fluid from synovium-like cells.","rn":[["","What lines the inside of a tendon sheath?","What lines the inside of a tendon sheath?","Flat lining cells; Like synovium intima"]]},{"id":11039,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","s":"Muscles with the same action on a joint tend to share what?","o":["The same nerve supply","The same origin","The same insertion","The same blood vessel","The same fibre type"],"e":"Skeletal muscle is supplied by somatic motor nerves, and <b>muscles with the same action on a joint tend to be innervated by the same nerve</b>.","w":["Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Same action → same nerve.","rn":[["","Muscles with the same joint action share what?","Muscles with the same joint action share what?","Usually the same nerve"],["","What is CN (cranial nerve) X?","What is CN (cranial nerve) X?","The vagus nerve; Parasympathetic supply to heart"]]},{"id":11040,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","f":"mu34","s":"Put the events at the neuromuscular junction in order.","o":["ACh released → Na⁺ channels open → muscle AP → contraction","ACh released → K⁺ channels open → muscle AP → contraction","Na⁺ channels open → ACh released → muscle AP → contraction","Muscle AP → ACh released → Na⁺ channels open → contraction","ACh released → muscle AP → Na⁺ channels open → contraction"],"e":"NMJ sequence: the nerve releases <b>acetylcholine (ACh)</b> → ACh <b>opens sodium channels</b> on the muscle membrane → elicits an <b>action potential</b> → <b>contraction</b>.","w":["Sodium.","ACh first.","Nerve first.","Channels then AP."],"k":"ACh → Na⁺ → AP → contraction.","rn":[["","NMJ: first 2 steps?","NMJ: first 2 steps?","1. ACh released; 2. Sodium channels open"]]},{"id":11041,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","s":"Name the specialised connection between a motor neuron and a skeletal muscle cell.","e":"The neuromuscular junction converts an electrical nerve signal into a chemical (ACh) signal and back to an electrical signal in the muscle.","k":"NMJ.","a":["neuromuscular junction","nmj","motor end plate","neuromuscular synapse"],"rn":[["","Neuromuscular junction","Neuromuscular junction","Specialised intercellular connection; Neuron to skeletal muscle cell"],["","Where does the motor nerve act on the muscle?","Where does the motor nerve act on the muscle?","Motor end plate; Neuromuscular junction"]]},{"id":11042,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","s":"How does the NMJ convert signals, in simple terms?","o":["Electrical → chemical (ACh) → electrical in the muscle","Chemical → electrical → chemical in the muscle","Electrical → electrical, with no chemical step","Electrical → mechanical → chemical in the muscle","Chemical (ATP) → electrical → mechanical"],"e":"The NMJ is a <b>chemical relay</b>: electrical (nerve) → chemical (ACh across the gap) → electrical (muscle action potential) → contraction.","w":["Electrical first.","Chemical relay.","Not mechanical.","ACh, not ATP."],"k":"NMJ: electrical → chemical → electrical."},{"id":11043,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"How does the activation of cardiac and some smooth muscle differ from skeletal muscle?","o":["Cardiac has Purkinje cells; some enteric smooth muscle has a plexus","Cardiac has NMJs; some enteric smooth muscle has Purkinje cells","Both use NMJs exactly as skeletal muscle does","Both use somatic motor nerves like skeletal muscle","Neither is innervated by any nerve"],"e":"By contrast with skeletal muscle's NMJ, <b>cardiac</b> cells have their own conducting <b>Purkinje cells</b>, and some <b>enteric smooth muscle</b> has a <b>specialist nerve plexus</b> in its walls.","w":["Swapped.","Different.","Autonomic.","They are."],"k":"Cardiac: Purkinje; enteric smooth: plexus.","rn":[["","What do some enteric smooth muscle cells have in their walls?","What do some enteric smooth muscle cells have in their walls?","A specialist plexus"]]},{"id":11044,"t":"Classification of Skeletal Muscle","s":"In which three ways can skeletal muscles be classified?","o":["By morphology, function and action","By colour, size and age","By nuclei, striations and branching","By nerve, artery and vein","By A band, I band and H zone"],"e":"Classification: <b>morphology</b> (shape, attachment, fibre orientation), <b>function</b> (agonist, antagonist, synergist, fixator) and <b>action</b> (flexion/extension, abduction/adduction, rotation).","w":["Not stated.","Types of muscle.","Not stated.","Sarcomere."],"k":"Morphology, function, action.","rn":[["","What are the 3 ways to classify skeletal muscle?","What are the 3 ways to classify skeletal muscle?","Morphology; Function; Action"]]},{"id":11045,"t":"Classification of Skeletal Muscle","s":"How is the sternocleidomastoid named?","o":["By its attachments","By its action","By its shape","By its fibre direction","By its nerve supply"],"e":"Muscles are often named by attachment, e.g. <b>sternocleidomastoid</b> (sternum, clavicle, mastoid), or by action, e.g. <b>extensor digiti minimi</b>.","w":["Extensor digiti minimi.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Muscle names from attachments or actions.","rn":[["","What 3 features define morphological classification?","What 3 features define morphological classification?","Shape; Attachment; Fibre orientation"],["","What does a flat parallel muscle attach by?","What does a flat parallel muscle attach by?","Aponeurosis"],["","What are the 3 types of attachment?","What are the 3 types of attachment?","Tendon; Aponeurosis; Raphe"]]},{"id":11046,"t":"Classification of Skeletal Muscle","s":"Rectus femoris has the arrangement shown in <b>B</b>. Compared with a parallel-fibred muscle of the same size, what is expected?","o":["More force, but less excursion","Less force, but more excursion","More force and more excursion","The same force and excursion","No force without a raphe"],"e":"B is bipennate (rectus femoris). Pennate muscles have more fibres per area, so more force, but each fibre is short and angled, so less excursion.","w":["Pennate muscles pack in more fibres – more force.","There is a trade-off: less shortening.","Fibre arrangement changes both.","Not relevant."],"k":"Pennate: more force, less excursion.","img":{"src":"11/pennate","cap":"Three arrangements of muscle fibres around a tendon. Names removed."}},{"id":11047,"t":"Classification of Skeletal Muscle","s":"Why are pennate muscles stronger than parallel-fibred muscles, and what is the trade-off?","o":["Bigger cross-sectional area, but less excursion","Bigger cross-sectional area, and more excursion","Longer fibres, and more excursion","Longer fibres, but less excursion","More nuclei per fibre, but less excursion"],"e":"<b>Pennate</b> muscles (fibres oblique, “like a feather”) are <b>stronger</b> because of a <b>bigger cross-sectional area</b>, but have <b>less excursion</b>. Parallel/fusiform muscles have long fibres: less force, more shortening.","w":["Less excursion.","Shorter fibres.","Shorter.","Not stated."],"k":"Pennate: more force, less excursion.","rn":[["","Why are pennate muscles stronger than parallel-fibre muscles?","Why are pennate muscles stronger than parallel-fibre muscles?","Bigger cross-sectional area"]]},{"id":11048,"t":"Classification of Skeletal Muscle","s":"Name the muscle part given as an example of a multipennate muscle.","e":"The acromial part of deltoid is multipennate.","k":"Deltoid (acromial).","a":["deltoid","acromial deltoid","acromial part of deltoid","deltoid acromial part","middle deltoid"],"rn":[["","Which multipennate muscle is a series of bipennate muscles?","Which multipennate muscle is a series of bipennate muscles?","Acromial part of deltoid"]]},{"id":11049,"t":"Classification of Skeletal Muscle","f":"mu39","s":"Which pairing of shape and muscle example is correct?","o":["Triangular – trapezius","Strap – biceps brachii","Fusiform – rhomboids","Two bellies – rectus femoris","Quadrilateral – sternohyoid"],"e":"Shapes: rhomboid (rhomboids); quadrilateral (quadratus lumborum/femoris/plantae); strap (thyrohyoid, omohyoid, sternohyoid, sternothyroid); fusiform (coracobrachialis, biceps brachii, gastrocnemius); two bellies (biceps femoris); two-headed (rectus femoris); <b>triangular (trapezius)</b>.","w":["Fusiform.","Rhomboid.","Two-headed; biceps femoris has two bellies.","Strap."],"k":"Shape examples.","rn":[["","Which muscle is triangular?","Which muscle is triangular?","Trapezius"]]},{"id":11050,"t":"Classification of Skeletal Muscle","s":"What is the prime mover producing a movement called?","e":"Agonist = prime mover; antagonist opposes and must relax; synergist assists; fixator stabilises.","k":"Agonist.","a":["agonist","prime mover"],"rn":[["","What is an agonist?","What is an agonist?","Prime mover"],["","What is an antagonist?","What is an antagonist?","Opposes the agonist; Relaxes before agonist contracts"]]},{"id":11051,"t":"Classification of Skeletal Muscle","f":"mu42","s":"During knee flexion by biceps femoris, what is the role of the quadriceps femoris?","o":["Antagonist – it must relax","Agonist – it produces the movement","Synergist – it prevents unwanted movement","Fixator – it stabilises isometrically","Agonist – it extends the hip"],"e":"When <b>biceps femoris</b> (hamstring) is the agonist flexing the knee, <b>quadriceps femoris</b> is the <b>antagonist</b> and must <b>relax</b>. Roles swap with the movement.","w":["Biceps femoris.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not this movement."],"k":"Roles depend on the movement.","rn":[["","Example of an antagonist","Example of an antagonist","Quadriceps femoris; When biceps femoris flexes knee"],["","What is an antagonist?","What is an antagonist?","Opposes the agonist; Relaxes before agonist contracts"]]},{"id":11052,"t":"Classification of Skeletal Muscle","f":"mu43","s":"When the long finger flexors and extensors act, extensor carpi radialis and flexor carpi radialis contract together to stop unwanted wrist movement. What are they acting as?","o":["Synergists","Agonists","Antagonists","Fixators of the scapula","Prime movers of the fingers"],"e":"<b>Synergists</b> prevent unwanted movements; the wrist muscles contract together to <b>stop unwanted wrist movement</b> while the finger muscles act. Synergist = Greek “working together”.","w":["Not prime movers.","Not opposing.","Different example.","The finger muscles."],"k":"Synergists prevent unwanted movement.","rn":[["","What is a synergist?","What is a synergist?","Prevents unwanted movements"],["","When do the carpi radialis muscles act as synergists?","When do the carpi radialis muscles act as synergists?","When long finger flexors/extensors act"]]},{"id":11053,"t":"Classification of Skeletal Muscle","f":"mu44","s":"Serratus anterior pulls the scapula forward while rhomboid pulls it back, holding it steady without movement as the arm moves. What role is this?","o":["Fixator – stabilising isometrically","Synergist – preventing unwanted movement","Agonist – producing the movement","Antagonist – opposing the movement","Prime mover – lifting the arm"],"e":"A <b>fixator</b> contracts <b>isometrically</b> (equal length) – increases tone but produces no movement – to stabilise, e.g. holding the <b>scapula</b> steady as a base for arm movement.","w":["Holding steady.","No movement.","Not opposing.","Not moving."],"k":"Fixator = isometric stabiliser.","rn":[["","What is a fixator?","What is a fixator?","Contracts isometrically; Increases tone; No movement"]]},{"id":11054,"t":"Classification of Skeletal Muscle","s":"What term describes a contraction with no change in muscle length?","e":"Isometric = equal measure: contraction without change in length.","k":"Isometric.","a":["isometric","isometric contraction"]},{"id":11055,"t":"Classification of Skeletal Muscle","s":"Which statement about functional roles is correct?","o":["Roles are not fixed between movements","Roles are fixed: each muscle has one role","Antagonists contract with agonists to add force","Fixators always produce movement","Synergists are the prime movers"],"e":"Roles are <b>not fixed</b>: e.g. quadriceps is the agonist in knee extension but the antagonist in knee flexion.","w":["Not fixed.","They relax.","No movement.","Agonists are."],"k":"Roles change with movement."},{"id":11056,"t":"Classification of Skeletal Muscle","s":"Which is the correct way to state a muscle's action?","o":["State the part moving and the joint","State the muscle and its nerve","State the origin and insertion only","State the fibre type and shape","State the muscle and its role"],"e":"Always <b>state which part is moving and at which joint</b>, e.g. “biceps brachii <b>flexes the forearm at the elbow joint</b>”; “quadriceps femoris <b>extends the leg at the knee joint</b>”.","w":["Part and joint.","Part and joint.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Action: part + joint.","rn":[["","When describing a muscle action, what must you state?","When describing a muscle action, what must you state?","Which part is moving; At which joint"]]},{"id":11057,"t":"Classification of Skeletal Muscle","f":"mu46","s":"Which muscles are elbow flexors?","o":["Biceps brachii, brachialis and brachioradialis","Biceps brachii, brachialis and triceps brachii","Triceps brachii and anconeus","Brachialis, brachioradialis and anconeus","Biceps brachii and coracobrachialis only"],"e":"Elbow flexors: <b>biceps brachii, brachialis, brachioradialis</b>. Elbow extensors: <b>triceps brachii, anconeus</b>.","w":["Triceps extends.","Extensors.","Anconeus extends.","Not listed."],"k":"Elbow flexors list.","rn":[["","What are the Elbow Flexors?","What are the Elbow Flexors?","Biceps brachii; Brachialis; Brachioradialis"]]},{"id":11058,"t":"Classification of Skeletal Muscle","f":"mu47","s":"Which group contains only hip adductors?","o":["Adductor magnus, longus, brevis and pectineus","Adductor magnus, longus, brevis and piriformis","Gluteus medius, gluteus minimus and TFL","Adductor longus, brevis, pectineus and gemelli","Piriformis, obturator internus and quadratus femoris"],"e":"Hip adductors: <b>adductor magnus, longus, brevis and pectineus</b>. Hip abductors: gluteus medius, minimus, TFL. Short lateral rotators: piriformis, gemelli, obturators, quadratus femoris.","w":["Piriformis is a lateral rotator.","Abductors.","Gemelli rotate.","Lateral rotators."],"k":"Hip adductors list.","rn":[["","Which 3 muscles form the adductor group?","Which 3 muscles form the adductor group?","Adductor brevis; Adductor longus; Adductor magnus"]]},{"id":11059,"t":"Classification of Skeletal Muscle","f":"mu48","s":"Which muscles are short lateral (external) rotators of the hip?","o":["Piriformis, gemelli, obturators, quadratus femoris","Gluteus medius, gluteus minimus and TFL","Adductor magnus, longus, brevis and pectineus","Piriformis, gemelli, obturators and pectineus","Rectus femoris, sartorius and gemelli"],"e":"Short lateral rotators of the hip: <b>piriformis, superior and inferior gemellus, obturator internus, quadratus femoris, obturator externus</b> – passing behind the hip to rotate the thigh laterally.","w":["Abductors.","Adductors.","Pectineus adducts.","Not stated."],"k":"Hip lateral rotators list.","rn":[["","Which 3 external hip rotators lie inferiorly?","Which 3 external hip rotators lie inferiorly?","Inferior gemellus; Quadratus femoris; External obturator"],["","Which 3 external hip rotators lie superiorly?","Which 3 external hip rotators lie superiorly?","Piriformis; Superior gemellus; Internal obturator"]]},{"id":11060,"t":"Classification of Skeletal Muscle","s":"Which muscles abduct the hip?","o":["Gluteus medius, gluteus minimus and TFL","Gluteus medius, piriformis and TFL","Adductor longus, adductor brevis and pectineus","Piriformis, gemelli and quadratus femoris","Gluteus minimus, pectineus and TFL"],"e":"Hip abductors: <b>gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, tensor fasciae latae (TFL)</b>.","w":["Piriformis rotates.","Adductors.","Rotators.","Pectineus adducts."],"k":"Hip abductors list.","rn":[["","Which muscles abduct the hip?","Which muscles abduct the hip?","Gluteus medius; Gluteus minimus; TFL (tensor fasciae latae)"]]},{"id":11061,"t":"Repair and Regeneration of Muscle","s":"Why can skeletal muscle regenerate after damage but cardiac muscle cannot?","o":["Skeletal has satellite cells; cardiac has none, so scars","Cardiac has satellite cells; skeletal has none, so scars","Both have satellite cells; cardiac uses them more slowly","Neither has satellite cells; both scar after damage","Skeletal has satellite cells; cardiac regenerates by hyperplasia"],"e":"<b>Skeletal</b> muscle has resident precursor cells – <b>satellite cells</b> – that divide to repair fibres. <b>Cardiac</b> muscle has <b>no resident stem cell population</b>; dead cells are replaced by a <b>collagenous scar</b>.","w":["Swapped.","Cardiac has none.","Skeletal regenerates.","Cardiac scars."],"k":"Skeletal repairs (satellite cells); cardiac scars.","rn":[["","Why can't cardiac muscle regenerate?","Why can't cardiac muscle regenerate?","No resident stem cells; Dead cells replaced by scar"]]},{"id":11062,"t":"Repair and Regeneration of Muscle","s":"Name the muscle stem cells found between the sarcolemma and the basal lamina.","e":"Satellite cells (between sarcolemma and basal lamina) are activated by trauma (e.g. resistance training) and enable skeletal muscle repair; cardiac muscle lacks them.","k":"Satellite cells.","a":["satellite cells","satellite cell","satellite"],"rn":[["","What activates satellite cells?","What activates satellite cells?","Trauma, damage or injury; e.g. resistance training overload"],["","Which layer of the basement membrane do satellite cells lie under?","Which layer of the basement membrane do satellite cells lie under?","Basal lamina (uppermost layer)"],["","Where are satellite cells found?","Where are satellite cells found?","Between sarcolemma; And basement membrane"]]},{"id":11063,"t":"Repair and Regeneration of Muscle","s":"What happens when satellite cells are activated by trauma such as resistance training?","o":["They proliferate, migrate and fuse with existing fibres","They proliferate and form many new separate fibres","They differentiate into cardiac cells to help the heart","They form a collagenous scar over the damage","They stay dormant until the fibre dies"],"e":"Usually <b>dormant</b>, satellite cells are <b>activated by damage</b>; they <b>proliferate</b>, daughter cells <b>migrate</b>, then <b>fuse with the existing fibre, donating their nuclei</b>. This increases the size and amount of <b>actin and myosin</b> within fibres – it does <b>not</b> create more fibres in humans.","w":["They fuse with existing fibres.","Not stated.","Cardiac scar.","They activate."],"k":"Satellite cells fuse → hypertrophy.","rn":[["","How do satellite cells help repair a muscle fibre?","How do satellite cells help repair a muscle fibre?","Fuse to the existing fibre; Donate their nuclei; Lay down new proteins"]]},{"id":11064,"t":"Repair and Regeneration of Muscle","s":"How long does satellite cell activation and proliferation last after a training stimulus?","o":["Up to 48 hours","Up to 6 hours","Up to 1 week","Up to 2 weeks","Up to 24 hours"],"e":"Satellite cell activation and proliferation last <b>up to 48 hours</b> after the training stimulus.","w":["48 h.","48 h.","48 h.","48 h."],"k":"Activation up to 48 h.","rn":[["","How long does satellite cell activation last after trauma?","How long does satellite cell activation last after trauma?","Up to 48 hours"]]},{"id":11065,"t":"Repair and Regeneration of Muscle","s":"Which fibre type has more satellite cells, and why?","o":["Type I – 5–6 times more, from more capillaries and use","Type II – 5–6 times more, from more capillaries and use","Type I – 2 times more, from more capillaries and use","Type II – 5–6 times more, from greater power output","Type I and II – equal numbers in both"],"e":"<b>Type I (slow-twitch, oxidative)</b> fibres have <b>5–6 times more</b> satellite cells than Type II, due to <b>greater blood/capillary supply</b> and <b>more frequent use</b> (more minor injuries).","w":["Type I.","5–6 times.","Type I.","Type I has more."],"k":"Type I: 5–6× more satellite cells.","rn":[["","Why do Type I fibres have more satellite cells?","Why do Type I fibres have more satellite cells?","Greater blood/capillary supply; Used most: more minor injuries"]]},{"id":11066,"t":"Repair and Regeneration of Muscle","f":"mu53","s":"Which statement correctly contrasts hypertrophy and hyperplasia in human muscle?","o":["Hypertrophy occurs with exercise; hyperplasia probably only in tumours","Hyperplasia occurs with exercise; hypertrophy probably only in tumours","Both hypertrophy and hyperplasia occur with exercise","Hypertrophy means more cells; hyperplasia means bigger cells","Neither hypertrophy nor hyperplasia occurs in human muscle"],"e":"<b>Hypertrophy</b>: increase in cell <b>size</b> → bigger mass and cross-sectional area, usually through <b>exercise</b>. <b>Hyperplasia</b>: increase in cell <b>number</b> – <b>probably does not occur normally</b>, only in <b>tumours</b>.","w":["Swapped.","Hyperplasia probably not.","Swapped.","Hypertrophy occurs."],"k":"Hypertrophy yes; hyperplasia only in tumours.","rn":[["","What is hyperplasia?","What is hyperplasia?","Increase in cell number; Only in tumours"],["","What is hypertrophy?","What is hypertrophy?","Increase in cell size; Usually through exercise"]]},{"id":11067,"t":"Repair and Regeneration of Muscle","s":"How do skeletal and cardiac muscle each adapt to regular increasing workloads?","o":["Cardiac squeezes blood out better; skeletal transmits force better","Cardiac transmits force better; skeletal squeezes blood better","Both adapt by increasing their number of cells","Cardiac regrows from satellite cells; skeletal does not adapt","Neither adapts to regular increasing workloads"],"e":"Both <b>adapt to regular increasing workloads</b>: cardiac muscle becomes more efficient at <b>squeezing blood out of its chambers</b>; skeletal muscle at <b>transmitting forces through its tendinous attachments</b>.","w":["Swapped.","Not hyperplasia.","No satellite cells.","Both adapt."],"k":"Workload adaptations.","rn":[["","How does cardiac muscle adapt to more workload?","How does cardiac muscle adapt to more workload?","Better at squeezing blood; Out of its chambers"]]},{"id":11068,"t":"Repair and Regeneration of Muscle","f":"mu54","s":"Which list gives the causes of muscle atrophy?","o":["Lack of exercise, poor nutrition, poor circulation, neurological, genetic","Too much exercise, poor nutrition, poor circulation, neurological, genetic","Lack of exercise, excess protein, good circulation, neurological, genetic","Satellite cell activation, poor nutrition, hyperplasia, genetic","Lack of exercise, poor nutrition, isometric contraction, genetic"],"e":"<b>Atrophy = wasting of muscle</b>. Causes: <b>lack of exercise, poor nutrition, poor circulation, neurological, genetic</b> (memory aid ENCNG).","w":["Lack of exercise.","Not listed.","Not listed.","Not listed."],"k":"Atrophy causes.","rn":[["","What are 3 lifestyle causes of atrophy?","What are 3 lifestyle causes of atrophy?","Lack of exercise; Poor nutrition; Poor circulation"],["","What are 2 other causes of atrophy?","What are 2 other causes of atrophy?","Neurological; Genetic; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":11069,"t":"Repair and Regeneration of Muscle","s":"A patient with a long-term limb nerve injury develops thin, wasted muscles. Name this change.","e":"Atrophy (decrease in size) can be neurological, from disuse, ageing and other causes.","k":"Atrophy.","a":["atrophy","muscle atrophy","neurogenic atrophy","wasting"],"rn":[["","What is atrophy?","What is atrophy?","Wasting of muscles"]]},{"id":11070,"t":"Repair and Regeneration of Muscle","s":"After a heart attack, the damaged myocardium is examined weeks later. What would you expect to find?","o":["A collagenous scar replacing dead cells","Regenerated muscle from satellite cells","Hyperplasia of surviving cardiac cells","Hypertrophy of the scar into new muscle","Unchanged tissue after repair"],"e":"Cardiac muscle has <b>no resident stem cells</b>; dead cells are <b>not replaced</b> but replaced by a <b>collagenous scar</b>.","w":["No satellite cells.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Scar."],"k":"Cardiac damage → scar.","rn":[["","Why can't cardiac muscle regenerate?","Why can't cardiac muscle regenerate?","No resident stem cells; Dead cells replaced by scar"]]},{"id":11071,"t":"Repair and Regeneration of Muscle","s":"A weightlifter's muscles grow over months. What is the main cellular change?","o":["Hypertrophy: fibres enlarge as satellite cells fuse","Hyperplasia: new fibres form from satellite cells","Hypertrophy: fibres divide into more fibres","Hyperplasia: fibres enlarge with more actin and more myosin","Atrophy: fibres shrink and are replaced"],"e":"Exercise causes <b>hypertrophy</b>; satellite cells fuse with existing fibres, increasing contractile proteins. Hyperplasia probably does not occur normally.","w":["Hyperplasia probably not.","Not divide.","Bigger cells = hypertrophy.","Grow."],"k":"Training → hypertrophy.","rn":[["","How do satellite cells help repair a muscle fibre?","How do satellite cells help repair a muscle fibre?","Fuse to the existing fibre; Donate their nuclei; Lay down new proteins"]]},{"id":11072,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"Which tissue would you expect in the wall of the gut and in the tunica media of arteries?","o":["Smooth muscle","Skeletal muscle","Cardiac muscle","Tendon","Fibrocartilage"],"e":"<b>Smooth muscle</b> is found in blood-vessel walls (<b>tunica media</b>) and in the walls of viscera (GI, genitourinary, respiratory).","w":["Body wall/limbs.","Heart/great vessels.","Attachments.","Joints."],"k":"Smooth muscle in vessels and viscera.","rn":[["","Where is smooth muscle found in vessels and skin?","Where is smooth muscle found in vessels and skin?","Blood vessel walls (tunica media); Hair follicles"],["","Which viscera contain smooth muscle?","Which viscera contain smooth muscle?","Gastrointestinal; Genitourinary; Respiratory; Urogenital"]]},{"id":11073,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"Cardiac muscle is found in the heart wall and in which other location?","o":["The walls of the great vessels","The tunica media of all arteries","The diaphragm","The wall of the oesophagus","Around hair follicles"],"e":"Cardiac muscle is found in the <b>heart wall (myocardium)</b> and the <b>walls of the great vessels</b>.","w":["Smooth.","Skeletal.","Not stated.","Smooth."],"k":"Cardiac: myocardium + great vessels.","rn":[["","Where is Cardiac Muscle found?","Where is Cardiac Muscle found?","Heart wall (myocardium); Great vessel walls"]]},{"id":11074,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"How are cardiac muscle fibres arranged?","o":["Whorls and spirals; branching shorter cells, central nuclei","Long parallel fibres; many peripheral nuclei","Whorls and spirals; long cells with peripheral nuclei","Isolated spindle cells with central nuclei","Branching longer cells with peripheral nuclei"],"e":"Cardiac muscle: a <b>branching network</b> of individual cells (shorter than skeletal) with <b>central nuclei</b>, arranged in <b>whorls and spirals</b>, linked by intercalated discs.","w":["Skeletal.","Central nuclei.","Smooth.","Shorter; central."],"k":"Cardiac arrangement.","rn":[["","How are cardiac fibres arranged?","How are cardiac fibres arranged?","Whorls and spirals"],["","Image card: What type of muscle is this? What section? How can you tell?","What type of muscle is this? What section? How can you tell?","Cardiac, transverse; Central irregular nuclei; Whorled fibres"],["","Image card: What type of muscle is being shown? How can you tell?","What type of muscle is being shown? How can you tell?","Cardiac muscle (longitudinal); Intercalated discs, central nuclei; Branching (anastomosing) cords"]]},{"id":11075,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"In a transverse section, which features suggest cardiac rather than skeletal muscle?","o":["Elliptical cells, central irregular nuclei, capillaries","Polygonal fibres with peripheral nuclei","Elliptical cells with peripheral regular nuclei and few capillaries","Concentric lamellae around a canal","Collagen fibres with flat inactive nuclei"],"e":"Cardiac transverse section: cells look <b>elliptical or lobulated</b>, with <b>centrally placed, irregular nuclei</b>, fibrocollagenous septa with small vessels and a <b>rich capillary supply</b>. Skeletal fibres look polygonal with peripheral nuclei.","w":["Skeletal.","Central irregular nuclei.","Bone.","Tendon."],"k":"Cardiac TS features.","rn":[["","Cardiac muscle in transverse section: cells and nuclei?","Cardiac muscle in transverse section: cells and nuclei?","Elliptical/lobulated cells; Central, irregular nuclei"]]},{"id":11076,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"In skeletal muscle histology, what do the labels N and S indicate?","o":["N = nuclei; S = septa","N = nerve; S = sarcomere","N = nuclei; S = sarcolemma","N = NMJ; S = septa","N = nuclei; S = satellite cells"],"e":"On the skeletal muscle histology images: <b>N = nuclei</b>, <b>S = fibrocollagenous septa</b>.","w":["Not stated.","Septa.","Nuclei.","Septa."],"k":"N = nuclei; S = septa.","rn":[["","Image card: What type of muscle is being shown? How can you tell?","What type of muscle is being shown? How can you tell?","Skeletal muscle (transverse); Polygonal fibres, peripheral nuclei; Fibrocollagenous septa (S)"]]},{"id":11077,"t":"Components of Muscle Tissue","s":"Which statement about the endomysium is correct?","o":["It wraps each fibre and is the innermost wrapping","It wraps each fascicle and is the middle wrapping","It wraps the whole muscle and is outermost","It is the plasma membrane of a muscle fibre","It wraps each myofibril within a fibre"],"e":"<b>Endomysium</b> (“endo” = within) surrounds <b>each individual muscle fibre</b> – the innermost wrapping. Satellite cells lie under the basement membrane near it.","w":["Perimysium.","Epimysium.","Sarcolemma.","SR and T-tubules."],"k":"Endomysium wraps each fibre.","rn":[["","What is the endomysium?","What is the endomysium?","Surrounds a muscle fibre; Innermost layer"]]},{"id":11078,"t":"Repair and Regeneration of Muscle","s":"Which statement best explains why satellite cells do not increase fibre number in humans?","o":["They fuse with existing fibres instead","They form new fibres only in cardiac muscle","They are destroyed by heavy exercise","They become Purkinje fibres after injury","They form new fibres only in tumours"],"e":"Satellite cells <b>fuse with the existing fibre, donating their nuclei</b>; this increases fibre size and contractile proteins but does <b>not create more fibres</b> in humans.","w":["Not cardiac.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Hyperplasia."],"k":"Satellite cells add to existing fibres.","rn":[["","How do satellite cells help repair a muscle fibre?","How do satellite cells help repair a muscle fibre?","Fuse to the existing fibre; Donate their nuclei; Lay down new proteins"]]},{"id":11079,"t":"Components of Muscle Tissue","s":"Which statement about the cell membrane relevant to muscle is correct?","o":["A lipid bilayer controlling ion flow; the sarcolemma","A lipid bilayer controlling ion flow; the sarcoplasm","A protein layer of actin controlling ion flow","A lipid bilayer that is absent in muscle cells","A connective-tissue layer; the endomysium"],"e":"The <b>cell membrane</b> is a <b>lipid bilayer</b> with <b>hydrophilic and hydrophobic</b> parts, controlling ion flow; in muscle it is the <b>sarcolemma</b>.","w":["Sarcolemma.","Lipid.","Present.","Membrane."],"k":"Membrane → sarcolemma.","rn":[["","Why can the cell membrane control ion flow?","Why can the cell membrane control ion flow?","Hydrophilic lipid ends; Hydrophobic lipid ends"],["","What is the cell membrane of a cell?","What is the cell membrane of a cell?","Surrounds each cell; A lipid bilayer; Has hydrophilic and hydrophobic parts"]]},{"id":11080,"t":"Components of Muscle Tissue","f":"mu13","s":"Inside a skeletal muscle fibre, what wraps each myofibril?","o":["T-tubules and SR","The perimysium and endomysium","The epimysium","Intercalated discs","Tendon sheaths"],"e":"The 3D view shows <b>T-tubules</b> and the <b>sarcoplasmic reticulum</b> wrapping each myofibril; the cross-section shows myofibrils in the sarcoplasm and a myosatellite cell under the endomysium.","w":["Fascicle/fibre wrappings.","Muscle.","Cardiac.","Tendons."],"k":"SR and T-tubules wrap myofibrils.","rn":[["","Image card: What is hidden under the ?","What is hidden under the ?","T-tubules"]]},{"id":11081,"t":"Components of Muscle Tissue","s":"Which statement about the origin of the word “sarco” is correct?","o":["Greek sarx, meaning flesh","Latin sarcina, meaning bundle","Greek sarx, meaning muscle fibre","Latin tendere, meaning stretch","Greek raphe, meaning seam"],"e":"<b>“Sarco”</b> comes from the Greek <b>sarx = flesh</b>: “the muscle version of…”.","w":["Not stated.","Flesh.","Tendon.","Raphe."],"k":"Sarco = flesh."},{"id":11082,"t":"Classification of Skeletal Muscle","s":"Name a muscle given as an example of a unipennate muscle.","e":"Unipennate: extensor digitorum longus; bipennate: rectus femoris; multipennate: acromial part of deltoid.","k":"Extensor digitorum longus.","a":["extensor digitorum longus","edl"],"rn":[["","Morphology examples: unipennate, bipennate, multipennate","Morphology examples: unipennate, bipennate, multipennate","Unipennate: extensor digitorum longus; Bipennate: rectus femoris; Multipennate: deltoid"],["","Name the extensor in the lower leg","Name the extensor in the lower leg","Extensor digitorum longus"]]},{"id":11083,"t":"Classification of Skeletal Muscle","s":"Which muscle is classified as having two bellies, and which as two-headed?","o":["Two bellies: biceps femoris; two-headed: rectus femoris","Two bellies: rectus femoris; two-headed: biceps femoris","Two bellies: biceps brachii; two-headed: rectus femoris","Two bellies: biceps femoris; two-headed: gastrocnemius","Two bellies: sartorius; two-headed: rectus femoris"],"e":"From the speaker notes: <b>two bellies – biceps femoris</b>; <b>two-headed – rectus femoris</b>.","w":["Swapped.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Two bellies vs two-headed examples.","rn":[["","Which muscle has two bellies?","Which muscle has two bellies?","Biceps femoris"],["","Which muscle is two-headed?","Which muscle is two-headed?","Rectus femoris"]]},{"id":11084,"t":"Classification of Skeletal Muscle","s":"A student describes the action of quadriceps femoris as “moves the leg”. How should they improve it?","o":["“Quadriceps femoris extends the leg at the knee joint”","“Quadriceps femoris flexes the leg at the knee joint”","“Quadriceps femoris extends the thigh at the hip joint”","“Quadriceps femoris extends the leg”","“Quadriceps femoris moves the knee joint”"],"e":"State the part moving <b>and</b> the joint: “quadriceps femoris <b>extends the leg at the knee joint</b>”.","w":["Extends.","Knee.","Joint missing.","Part missing."],"k":"Precise action statements.","rn":[["","Give 2 correct muscle action statements","Give 2 correct muscle action statements","Biceps brachii flexes forearm, elbow; Quadriceps femoris extends leg, knee"]]},{"id":11085,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","s":"Which statement about the NMJ neurotransmitter and ion is correct?","o":["ACh opens sodium channels on the muscle membrane","ACh opens potassium channels on the muscle membrane","ACh opens sodium channels on the nerve terminal","Noradrenaline opens sodium channels on the muscle membrane","ATP opens calcium channels on the muscle membrane"],"e":"The nerve releases <b>acetylcholine</b>, which <b>opens sodium channels</b>, eliciting an action potential and contraction.","w":["Sodium.","Muscle.","ACh.","ACh, sodium."],"k":"ACh → Na⁺ channels.","rn":[["","NMJ: first 2 steps?","NMJ: first 2 steps?","1. ACh released; 2. Sodium channels open"]]},{"id":11086,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","s":"Which statement about the motor point and motor end plates is correct?","o":["The nerve enters at the motor point and branches to end plates","The nerve enters at the insertion and branches to end plates","The nerve enters at the motor point and ends at the tendon","The nerve enters at the motor point; spindles are motor","End plates are found mainly in cardiac muscle"],"e":"The motor nerve enters the muscle at the <b>motor point</b> and branches to <b>motor end plates</b>; <b>muscle spindles and tendon endings</b> send sensory information back.","w":["Motor point.","End plates on fibres.","Spindles are sensory.","Skeletal."],"k":"Motor point and end plates.","rn":[["","Where does the motor nerve enter the muscle?","Where does the motor nerve enter the muscle?","Motor point"]]},{"id":11087,"t":"Repair and Regeneration of Muscle","s":"One motor neuron's axon branches to end on several muscle fibres. What does this show?","o":["One neuron supplies several fibres via several NMJs","Each fibre is supplied by its own separate motor neuron","Several neurons supply one fibre via one NMJ","One fibre supplies several neurons","Only cardiac fibres have NMJs"],"e":"The NMJ figure shows <b>one motor neuron</b> with axon branches ending on <b>several muscle fibres</b>.","w":["Not shown.","Not shown.","Reversed.","Skeletal."],"k":"One neuron → several fibres.","rn":[["","Why do Type I fibres have more satellite cells?","Why do Type I fibres have more satellite cells?","Greater blood/capillary supply; Used most: more minor injuries"]]},{"id":11088,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","s":"Which statement about the Achilles tendon is correct?","o":["The largest tendon, from the calf to the calcaneum","The largest tendon, from the calf to the tibia","The largest aponeurosis, from the calf to the calcaneum","The largest raphe, from the calf to the calcaneum","The largest tendon, from the thigh to the patella"],"e":"The <b>largest tendon is the Achilles</b> (calcaneal) tendon, connecting the <b>calf muscles</b> to the <b>calcaneum</b>.","w":["Calcaneum.","Tendon.","Tendon.","Calf."],"k":"Achilles = largest tendon.","rn":[["","What does the Achilles tendon connect?","What does the Achilles tendon connect?","Calf muscles to calcaneum"],["","What is the largest tendon?","What is the largest tendon?","Achilles (calcaneal) tendon"]]},{"id":11089,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","s":"The broad flat tendinous sheet over the skull is an example of what kind of attachment (not a raphe)?","e":"An aponeurosis is a broad flat sheet of tendon (e.g. epicranial aponeurosis); a raphe is a seam of fused ends of two sheets.","k":"Aponeurosis.","a":["aponeurosis","epicranial aponeurosis"],"rn":[["","What are the 3 types of attachment?","What are the 3 types of attachment?","Tendon; Aponeurosis; Raphe"],["","What does a flat parallel muscle attach by?","What does a flat parallel muscle attach by?","Aponeurosis"],["","Aponeurosis","Aponeurosis","Thin sheet of connective tissue; Broad anchorage to bone"]]},{"id":11090,"t":"Classification of Skeletal Muscle","s":"Which pairing of muscle shape example and shape is correct?","o":["Orbicularis oculi – circular; pectoralis major – convergent","Orbicularis oculi – convergent; pectoralis major – circular","Biceps brachii – multipennate; deltoid – fusiform","Rectus femoris – unipennate; deltoid – multipennate","Sartorius – circular; pectoralis major – convergent"],"e":"Shapes on the body: <b>circular</b> (orbicularis oculi), fusiform (biceps brachii), <b>convergent</b> (pectoralis major), multipennate (deltoid), bipennate (rectus femoris), unipennate (EDL), quadrate/strap with tendinous intersections (rectus abdominis), thin parallel (sartorius).","w":["Swapped.","Swapped.","Bipennate.","Not stated."],"k":"Shape examples on the body.","rn":[["","Morphology examples: convergent, quadrate","Morphology examples: convergent, quadrate","Convergent: pectoralis major; Quadrate: rectus abdominis"],["","Morphology examples: fusiform, circular, digastric","Morphology examples: fusiform, circular, digastric","Fusiform: biceps brachii; Circular: orbicularis oculi; Digastric: omohyoid"]]},{"id":11091,"t":"Components of Muscle Tissue","s":"Name the connective-tissue layer labelled <b>A</b>, which surrounds the entire muscle.","e":"Epimysium surrounds the entire muscle; perimysium surrounds each bundle (fascicle); endomysium surrounds each muscle fibre.","k":"A = epimysium.","img":{"src":"11/sheaths","cap":"Cross-section of a skeletal muscle. Labels removed."},"a":["epimysium"],"rn":[["","What is the epimysium?","What is the epimysium?","Surrounds the entire muscle; Outermost layer"],["","Image card: What is hidden under the ?","What is hidden under the ?","Epimysium"]]},{"id":11092,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"Which statement about skeletal muscle's role in posture is correct?","o":["It moves bones and supports posture","Smooth muscle maintains posture","Cardiac muscle supports posture","Tendons alone maintain posture","Posture is maintained mainly by smooth muscle"],"e":"Skeletal muscle <b>moves bones</b> and <b>provides support to the body for posture</b>, and makes up the majority of muscle.","w":["Skeletal.","Skeletal.","With muscle.","Skeletal."],"k":"Skeletal muscle: movement and posture.","rn":[["","What does skeletal muscle do?","What does skeletal muscle do?","Powerful contractions; Moves bones; Supports posture"]]},{"id":11093,"t":"Classification of Skeletal Muscle","s":"Which explanation of the antagonist's behaviour is correct?","o":["It must relax before the agonist contracts","It contracts with the agonist to produce the movement","It always contracts isometrically","It prevents unwanted movements at another joint","It is the prime mover"],"e":"<b>Antagonist</b>: opposes the agonist and <b>must relax</b> before the agonist contracts.","w":["Agonist.","Fixator.","Synergist.","Agonist."],"k":"Antagonist relaxes.","rn":[["","What is an antagonist?","What is an antagonist?","Opposes the agonist; Relaxes before agonist contracts"]]},{"id":11094,"t":"Components of Muscle Tissue","s":"Name the layer labelled <b>B</b>, which wraps each bundle of fibres.","e":"B is the perimysium, which surrounds each fascicle (bundle of muscle fibres).","k":"B = perimysium.","img":{"src":"11/sheaths","cap":"Cross-section of a skeletal muscle. Labels removed."},"a":["perimysium"],"rn":[["","Image card: What is hidden under the ?","What is hidden under the ?","Perimysium"],["","What is the perimysium?","What is the perimysium?","Surrounds a fascicle; Middle layer"]]},{"id":11095,"t":"Intro to the Muscular System","s":"Which statement about ATP is correct?","o":["A coenzyme for energy transfer in sliding","A neurotransmitter released at the NMJ","A structural protein of the A band","A coenzyme for energy transfer stored in tendons","A protein that holds filaments in register"],"e":"<b>ATP (adenosine triphosphate)</b> is a <b>coenzyme that helps energy transfer</b> – the energy currency powering actin–myosin sliding.","w":["ACh.","Myosin.","Not stored in tendons.","Accessory proteins."],"k":"ATP = energy currency.","rn":[["","What is ATP?","What is ATP?","A coenzyme; Helps energy transfer"]]},{"id":11096,"t":"Components of Muscle Tissue","s":"Which statement best describes the cytoplasm and its muscle equivalent?","o":["Inside of the cell outside the nucleus; sarcoplasm","Membrane of the cell; sarcolemma","Sacs for protein transport; sarcoplasmic reticulum","Contractile unit of the cell; sarcomere","Inside of the cell outside the nucleus; sarcolemma"],"e":"<b>Cytoplasm</b>: the inside of the cell, external to the nucleus; in muscle, <b>sarcoplasm</b>.","w":["Different term.","ER.","Sarcomere.","Sarcoplasm."],"k":"Cytoplasm = sarcoplasm.","rn":[["","What is the cytoplasm of a cell?","What is the cytoplasm of a cell?","Inside of the cell; External to (outside) the nucleus"]]},{"id":11097,"t":"Components of Muscle Tissue","s":"What is the function of the endoplasmic reticulum in a generic cell, and its muscle name?","o":["Protein/lipid transport and synthesis; sarcoplasmic reticulum","Sacs for protein and lipid transport and synthesis; sarcoplasm","Contractile unit; sarcoplasmic reticulum","Cell boundary; sarcolemma","Energy currency; ATP"],"e":"<b>Endoplasmic reticulum</b>: a series of sacs for <b>transport and synthesis of proteins and lipids</b>; in muscle, the <b>sarcoplasmic reticulum</b>.","w":["Sarcoplasm is cytoplasm.","Sarcomere.","Membrane.","Not ER."],"k":"ER = sarcoplasmic reticulum.","rn":[["","What does the endoplasmic reticulum do?","What does the endoplasmic reticulum do?","Transports proteins and lipids; Synthesises proteins and lipids"]]},{"id":11098,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"Which pair of muscle types are striated?","o":["Skeletal and cardiac","Skeletal and smooth","Cardiac and smooth","Smooth only","All three"],"e":"<b>Skeletal</b> and <b>cardiac</b> muscle are striated; smooth muscle has no stripes.","w":["Smooth is not.","Smooth is not.","Not striated.","Smooth is not."],"k":"Striated: skeletal + cardiac.","rn":[["","What are the 3 types of muscle?","What are the 3 types of muscle?","Skeletal; Cardiac; Smooth"]]},{"id":11099,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"Which pair of muscle types are involuntary?","o":["Cardiac and smooth","Skeletal and cardiac","Skeletal and smooth","Only skeletal","All three"],"e":"<b>Cardiac</b> and <b>smooth</b> muscle are <b>involuntary</b>; skeletal is voluntary.","w":["Skeletal is voluntary.","Skeletal is voluntary.","Voluntary.","Skeletal is voluntary."],"k":"Involuntary: cardiac + smooth.","rn":[["","What are the 3 types of muscle?","What are the 3 types of muscle?","Skeletal; Cardiac; Smooth"]]},{"id":11100,"t":"Components of Muscle Tissue","s":"Layer <b>C</b> surrounds individual fibres. A disease damages only this layer. Which structures does it carry closest to each fibre?","o":["Capillaries and nerve branches reaching each muscle fibre","The main nerve trunk entering the whole muscle","The tendon sheath and synovial fluid","Satellite cells between sarcolemma and basal lamina","The intercalated discs"],"e":"C is the endomysium, the connective tissue around each fibre. Blood vessels and nerves pass in through the epimysium and perimysium and reach individual fibres in the endomysium.","w":["The main trunk enters through the epimysium.","Tendon sheaths surround long tendons, not fibres.","Satellite cells lie under the basal lamina, inside the endomysium.","Intercalated discs are in cardiac muscle."],"k":"C = endomysium; carries vessels and nerves to fibres.","img":{"src":"11/sheaths","cap":"Cross-section of a skeletal muscle. Labels removed."},"rn":[["","What is the endomysium?","What is the endomysium?","Surrounds a muscle fibre; Innermost layer"]]},{"id":11101,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"Which statement about where smooth muscle is associated in the skin is correct?","o":["It is associated with hair follicles","It forms part of the epidermis","It forms part of the dermis","It forms the sweat glands","It is absent from the skin"],"e":"Smooth muscle is found associated with <b>hair follicles</b> in the skin (among other sites).","w":["Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Hair follicles."],"k":"Smooth muscle at hair follicles.","rn":[["","Where is smooth muscle found in vessels and skin?","Where is smooth muscle found in vessels and skin?","Blood vessel walls (tunica media); Hair follicles"]]},{"id":11102,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","s":"Which pairing of zone and filament content is correct?","o":["H zone – only thick filaments","H zone – only thin filaments","I band – thick and thin overlap","A band – only thin filaments","Z line – only myosin"],"e":"<b>H zone</b> – only thick; <b>I band</b> – only thin (no overlap); <b>A band</b> – thick, including overlap; Z line anchors thin filaments; M line centre of H.","w":["Thick.","Thin only.","Thick + overlap.","Anchors thin."],"k":"Zone filament content.","rn":[["","H Zone","H Zone","Pale area; Centre of the A band; No thin filament overlap"],["","Which filaments make the dark and light zones?","Which filaments make the dark and light zones?","Dark: thick myosin; Light: thin actin"],["","A Band","A Band","Dark band; Anisotropic; Thick filaments (including overlap)"]]},{"id":11103,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","s":"A muscle contracts strongly. Which change would you see in the H zone?","o":["It narrows or disappears","It widens","It stays the same","It becomes an I band","It splits at the M line"],"e":"During contraction the <b>H zone shortens/disappears</b> as thin filaments slide towards the centre.","w":["Narrows.","Changes.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"H zone narrows."},{"id":11104,"t":"Components of Muscle Tissue","s":"Which statement about the epimysium, perimysium and endomysium at the ends of a muscle is correct?","o":["They merge at the ends to form the tendon","They merge at the ends to form the aponeurosis only","They end at the motor point","They become cartilage at the ends","They form the sarcomere at the ends"],"e":"The guide's analogy: these wrappings <b>merge at the ends to form the tendon</b>.","w":["Tendon.","Nerve entry.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Wrappings merge into tendon."},{"id":11105,"t":"Classification of Skeletal Muscle","s":"Which combination correctly identifies an elbow extensor and its antagonist during extension?","o":["Triceps brachii (agonist); biceps brachii (antagonist)","Biceps brachii (agonist); triceps brachii (antagonist)","Triceps brachii (agonist); anconeus (antagonist)","Brachialis (agonist); biceps brachii (antagonist)","Triceps brachii (antagonist); biceps brachii (agonist)"],"e":"During elbow <b>extension</b>, <b>triceps brachii</b> (with anconeus) is the agonist; the <b>flexors</b> such as biceps brachii are antagonists and must relax.","w":["Flexion.","Anconeus helps extend.","Brachialis flexes.","Flexion."],"k":"Elbow extension: triceps agonist.","rn":[["","What are the Elbow Extensors?","What are the Elbow Extensors?","Triceps brachii; Anconeus"],["","What is an antagonist?","What is an antagonist?","Opposes the agonist; Relaxes before agonist contracts"]]},{"id":11106,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"Which statement about skeletal muscle making up the body is correct?","o":["Skeletal muscle makes up the majority","Smooth muscle makes up the majority","Cardiac muscle makes up the majority","All three types are equal","Muscle is a small fraction of the body"],"e":"Skeletal muscle makes up the <b>majority of muscle</b> in the body.","w":["Skeletal.","Skeletal.","Skeletal.","Not stated."],"k":"Skeletal = majority.","rn":[["","How much of the body's muscle is skeletal?","How much of the body's muscle is skeletal?","The majority"]]},{"id":11107,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","s":"Which best explains why a tendon is strong in tension?","o":["Packed longitudinal collagen","Loosely arranged elastin fibres","Many peripheral nuclei","Intercalated discs between cells","Branching collagen fibres"],"e":"Tendons have <b>tightly packed, longitudinally running collagen fibres</b> – strong in tension.","w":["Not the reason.","Not the reason.","Cardiac.","Parallel."],"k":"Parallel collagen → tensile strength.","rn":[["","What does a tendon look like on histology?","What does a tendon look like on histology?","Tightly packed collagen fibres; Running longitudinally; Elongated flattened nuclei"]]},{"id":11108,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","s":"Which statement about the Latin origin of “tendon” is correct?","o":["Latin tendere – to stretch outward","Greek sarx – flesh","Greek synergos – working together","Greek raphe – seam","Latin agonista – athlete"],"e":"<b>Tendon</b> – from Latin <i>tendere</i>, to extend outward or stretch.","w":["Sarco.","Synergist.","Raphe.","Agonist."],"k":"Tendon etymology."},{"id":11109,"t":"Classification of Skeletal Muscle","s":"A muscle contracts to stabilise a joint without producing movement while another segment moves. Name its role.","e":"A fixator stabilises a proximal segment so another can move, e.g. serratus anterior and rhomboid holding the scapula steady.","k":"Fixator.","a":["fixator","fixer","stabiliser","stabilizer"],"rn":[["","Example of a Fixator","Example of a Fixator","Serratus anterior and rhomboid; Holding the scapula steady"]]},{"id":11110,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"Which statement about the somatic and autonomic nervous system in relation to muscle is correct?","o":["Somatic: voluntary skeletal muscle; autonomic: involuntary processes","Somatic: involuntary processes; autonomic: voluntary skeletal muscle","Somatic and autonomic: both supply skeletal muscle only","Somatic: smooth muscle; autonomic: skeletal muscle","Somatic: cardiac muscle; autonomic: skeletal muscle"],"e":"<b>Somatic</b> nerves supply <b>voluntary skeletal</b> muscle; <b>autonomic</b> nerves supply involuntary processes (heart rate, BP, respiration, digestion, urinary, sexual arousal).","w":["Swapped.","Not only.","Swapped.","Not stated."],"k":"Somatic vs autonomic supply.","rn":[["","What do somatic nerves supply?","What do somatic nerves supply?","Voluntary skeletal muscles"],["","What do autonomic nerves supply?","What do autonomic nerves supply?","Involuntary physiological processes"],["","Skeletal Muscle: Control, Appearance, Association?","Skeletal Muscle: Control, Appearance, Association?","Voluntary; Striated; Body wall (somatic)"]]},{"id":11111,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"Name the precursor cells that fuse in their hundreds to form one skeletal muscle fibre.","e":"Myoblasts fuse to form skeletal muscle fibres, explaining their many nuclei.","k":"Myoblasts.","a":["myoblasts","myoblast"],"rn":[["","How does a skeletal muscle cell form?","How does a skeletal muscle cell form?","Fusion of hundreds; Of precursor cells (myoblasts)"]]},{"id":11112,"t":"Components of Muscle Tissue","s":"Which statement about myofibrils is correct?","o":["Hundreds per fibre, each a chain of sarcomeres","One myofibril per fibre, made of a chain of sarcomeres","Hundreds of myofibrils per fascicle, each made of a single sarcomere","Myofibrils are the wrappings around each fibre","Myofibrils are chains of collagen in tendons"],"e":"Any muscle fibre has <b>hundreds of myofibrils</b> running parallel; a myofibril is a chain of sarcomeres.","w":["Hundreds.","Per fibre; chains.","Not wrappings.","Not collagen."],"k":"Myofibrils = sarcomere chains.","rn":[["","Myofibril","Myofibril","Chain of sarcomeres; Runs along a muscle fibre"]]},{"id":11113,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","s":"Which statement about why satellite cells are named “satellite” is correct?","o":["They sit on the outer surface of the fibre","They sit inside the fibre around the nucleus","They sit in the heart around the Purkinje fibres","They sit in tendons near the fibrocytes","They sit around each fascicle"],"e":"Named “satellite” because they sit on the <b>outer surface</b> of the fibre, between the <b>sarcolemma and basal lamina</b>.","w":["Outside.","Not cardiac.","Not tendons.","Under the basal lamina."],"k":"Satellite position.","rn":[["","How is the epitendineum covered?","How is the epitendineum covered?","Flat lining cells; On its outer surface"]]},{"id":11114,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","s":"Which is the best application of “muscles with the same action share a nerve”?","o":["Elbow flexors at the front of the arm share a nerve","Antagonist muscles always share a nerve","Every muscle has its own unique nerve","All muscles of a limb share one nerve","Cardiac muscle shares the musculocutaneous nerve"],"e":"Muscles with the <b>same action on a joint</b> tend to share the <b>same nerve</b> (e.g. biceps brachii is supplied by the musculocutaneous nerve).","w":["Same action.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not cardiac."],"k":"Same action, same nerve.","rn":[["","Muscles with the same joint action share what?","Muscles with the same joint action share what?","Usually the same nerve"]]},{"id":11115,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"Which statement about smooth muscle contraction is correct?","o":["Capable of slow, sustained contractions","Capable of fast, powerful contractions","Capable of fast contractions, then fatigue","Capable of voluntary, sustained contractions","Capable of fatigue-resistant, striated contraction"],"e":"Smooth muscle: <b>slow, sustained contractions</b>; involuntary, non-striated, central nucleus.","w":["Skeletal.","Skeletal.","Involuntary.","Cardiac."],"k":"Smooth: slow, sustained.","rn":[["","Key Features of Smooth Muscle","Key Features of Smooth Muscle","No stripes; Elongated, spindle-shaped fibres; Slow, sustained contractions"]]},{"id":11116,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"Name the dark cross-lines that join cardiac muscle cells end to end.","e":"Intercalated discs join cardiac cells end to end; with central nuclei they distinguish cardiac from skeletal muscle.","k":"Intercalated discs.","a":["intercalated discs","intercalated disc","intercalated disks","intercalated disk"],"rn":[["","Cardiac muscle in longitudinal section?","Cardiac muscle in longitudinal section?","Anastomosing cords of cells; Join at intercalated discs"],["","Intercalated Disc","Intercalated Disc","Links cardiac muscle cells; Electrically and mechanically; Seen as dark lines"],["","How do you tell cardiac from skeletal muscle on histology?","How do you tell cardiac from skeletal muscle on histology?","Central nucleus; Intercalated discs; Branching"]]},{"id":11117,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"The vagus nerve provides parasympathetic supply to which muscle type in the heart?","e":"Cardiac muscle receives autonomic supply (vagus = parasympathetic).","k":"Cardiac muscle.","a":["cardiac","cardiac muscle","myocardium"],"rn":[["","What are the 3 types of muscle?","What are the 3 types of muscle?","Skeletal; Cardiac; Smooth"],["","What nerves innervate Cardiac Muscle?","What nerves innervate Cardiac Muscle?","Visceral motor nerves; Parasympathetic (vagus); Sympathetic trunk"],["","Image card: What is hidden under the ?","What is hidden under the ?","Cardiac muscle cell"]]},{"id":11118,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","s":"A patient's biceps brachii is paralysed. Which nerve is most likely injured, and which movement is weakened?","o":["Musculocutaneous; elbow flexion and supination","Musculocutaneous; elbow extension and pronation","Radial; elbow flexion and supination","Median; elbow flexion and pronation","Ulnar; elbow flexion and supination"],"e":"Biceps brachii is innervated by the <b>musculocutaneous nerve</b>; it flexes the elbow and shoulder and aids <b>supination</b>.","w":["Flexion.","Musculocutaneous.","Musculocutaneous.","Musculocutaneous."],"k":"Biceps: musculocutaneous.","rn":[["","What is the Action of Biceps Brachii?","What is the Action of Biceps Brachii?","Flexes forearm at elbow; Flexes arm at shoulder; Aids supination of forearm"]]},{"id":11119,"t":"Classification of Skeletal Muscle","s":"Which labelling of muscles A, B and C is correct?","o":["A unipennate; B bipennate; C multipennate","A bipennate; B unipennate; C multipennate","A unipennate; B multipennate; C bipennate","A parallel; B fusiform; C convergent","A multipennate; B bipennate; C unipennate"],"e":"Unipennate: fibres attach on one side of a tendon (extensor digitorum longus). Bipennate: both sides, like a feather (rectus femoris). Multipennate: several tendon branches (acromial deltoid). More fibres fit in: more force, less excursion (shortening).","w":["B has fibres on both sides of a central tendon like a feather: bipennate.","C has several tendon branches: multipennate.","These are pennate arrangements, not parallel shapes.","Reversed."],"k":"Uni, bi, multi = one side, both, several.","img":{"src":"11/pennate","cap":"Three arrangements of muscle fibres around a tendon. Names removed."},"rn":[["","Morphology examples: unipennate, bipennate, multipennate","Morphology examples: unipennate, bipennate, multipennate","Unipennate: extensor digitorum longus; Bipennate: rectus femoris; Multipennate: deltoid"],["","What are the 3 types of Pennate Muscle?","What are the 3 types of Pennate Muscle?","Unipennate; Bipennate; Multipennate"]]},{"id":11120,"t":"Components of Muscle Tissue","s":"Which combination describes how skeletal muscle is organised from largest to smallest?","o":["Muscle → fascicle → fibre → myofibril → sarcomere → filaments","Muscle → fibre → fascicle → myofibril → sarcomere → filaments","Muscle → fascicle → myofibril → fibre → sarcomere → filaments","Fascicle → muscle → fibre → myofibril → sarcomere → filaments","Muscle → fascicle → fibre → sarcomere → myofibril → filaments"],"e":"Largest to smallest: <b>muscle</b> (epimysium) → <b>fascicle</b> (perimysium) → <b>fibre</b> (endomysium) → <b>myofibril</b> → <b>sarcomere</b> → <b>actin and myosin</b> filaments.","w":["Fascicle before fibre.","Fibre before myofibril.","Muscle first.","Myofibril before sarcomere."],"k":"Muscle hierarchy.","rn":[["","Myofibril","Myofibril","Chain of sarcomeres; Runs along a muscle fibre"],["","How many myofibrils are in one muscle fibre?","How many myofibrils are in one muscle fibre?","Hundreds; Running parallel along its length"],["","Muscle Fascicle","Muscle Fascicle","A bundle of muscle fibres"]]},{"id":11121,"t":"Repair and Regeneration of Muscle","s":"A 60-year-old man has a heart attack that kills part of his ventricular muscle. In the same month, his 25-year-old son tears a thigh muscle playing football. Months later, the son's thigh has fully repaired with new muscle, while the father's heart shows a permanent patch of non-contracting tissue.<br><br>Which explanation accounts for the difference?","o":["Skeletal muscle has satellite cells; cardiac muscle has none, so it scars","Cardiac muscle has satellite cells, but they cannot reach the heart wall","Both have satellite cells, but the heart is too old to use them","Skeletal muscle regenerates by hyperplasia, which also happens in the heart in adults after a heart attack","Cardiac muscle is replaced by smooth muscle after injury"],"e":"Step 1: skeletal muscle has resident muscle precursor cells called satellite cells, located between the sarcolemma and basement membrane, which divide to form new muscle cells and repair fibres. Step 2: cardiac muscle has no resident stem cell population, so dead cells are not replaced and are instead replaced by a collagenous scar.","w":["Cardiac muscle has no resident stem cell population.","The difference is that cardiac muscle lacks satellite cells, not age.","Hyperplasia probably does not occur normally, only in tumours.","Dead cardiac cells are replaced by a collagenous scar."],"k":"Skeletal muscle repairs via satellite cells; cardiac muscle scars.","rn":[["","Why can't cardiac muscle regenerate?","Why can't cardiac muscle regenerate?","No resident stem cells; Dead cells replaced by scar"]]},{"id":11122,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"A histology student examines three tissue samples. Sample A has stripes and many nuclei pushed to the edges of long cells. Sample B has stripes, a single central nucleus per cell, branching cells and dark lines between cells. Sample C has no stripes and spindle-shaped cells with a central, cigar-shaped nucleus.<br><br>Which samples are which, and which is controlled voluntarily?","o":["A skeletal (voluntary); B cardiac; C smooth","A cardiac (voluntary); B skeletal; C smooth","A smooth; B cardiac (voluntary); C skeletal","A skeletal; B smooth; C cardiac (voluntary)","A skeletal; B cardiac; C smooth, and all three are controlled voluntarily by somatic motor nerves"],"e":"Step 1: stripes with many peripheral nuclei identify skeletal muscle; stripes with a central nucleus, branching and dark intercalated discs identify cardiac muscle; no stripes with spindle cells and a central nucleus identify smooth muscle. Step 2: only skeletal muscle is voluntary; cardiac and smooth muscle are involuntary.","w":["Peripheral nuclei mean skeletal, and cardiac muscle is involuntary.","Sample A has stripes, so it is not smooth muscle.","Branching cells with intercalated discs are cardiac, not smooth.","Cardiac and smooth muscle are involuntary."],"k":"Skeletal: striated, peripheral nuclei, voluntary; cardiac: intercalated discs; smooth: spindle.","rn":[["","What are the 3 types of muscle?","What are the 3 types of muscle?","Skeletal; Cardiac; Smooth"],["","How do you tell cardiac from skeletal muscle on histology?","How do you tell cardiac from skeletal muscle on histology?","Central nucleus; Intercalated discs; Branching"]]},{"id":11123,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","s":"A researcher uses electron microscopy to compare a relaxed skeletal muscle fibre with the same fibre after a strong contraction. She measures the sarcomere, the A band, the I band, the H zone and the length of the actin and myosin filaments themselves.<br><br>Which set of findings is expected after contraction?","o":["Sarcomere, I band and H zone shorten; A band and filament lengths unchanged","All bands and both filaments shorten by about one-third","The A band shortens; the I band and H zone stay the same","The filaments shorten; the bands stay the same","The sarcomere lengthens as the Z lines move apart, while the A band shortens to the same extent"],"e":"Step 1: contraction works by sliding filaments: the thin (actin) and thick (myosin) filaments slide over each other without changing their own length. Step 2: so the Z lines move closer and the sarcomere, I band and H zone shorten, while the A band (the length of the thick filaments) stays the same.","w":["The filaments do not shorten; they slide.","The A band stays the same, and the I band and H zone shorten.","Filament lengths do not change.","The Z lines move closer and the A band does not change."],"k":"Contraction: sarcomere, I, H shorten; A band and filaments unchanged.","rn":[["","Which bands change during contraction?","Which bands change during contraction?","H zone, I band shorten; A band stays same; MNEMONIC"],["","What happens to the sarcomere during contraction?","What happens to the sarcomere during contraction?","Shortens"],["","H Zone","H Zone","Pale area; Centre of the A band; No thin filament overlap"]]},{"id":11124,"t":"Skeletal Muscle Structure and Function","s":"A 35-year-old woman is given a drug in an experiment that stops sodium channels on the muscle membrane from opening. Her motor nerves still fire normally and release their neurotransmitter, but her muscles do not contract.<br><br>At which step of the sequence at the neuromuscular junction is the block, and why does contraction fail?","o":["ACh cannot open Na⁺ channels, so no muscle action potential is triggered","The nerve cannot release ACh, so there is no signal","ATP is not used by actin, so myosin cannot slide","The action potential forms but cannot cause contraction because of a lack of satellite cells","The sarcolemma has no sodium channels, as these are only found in nerve cells and never in muscle"],"e":"Step 1: at the neuromuscular junction, the nerve releases acetylcholine (ACh), which opens sodium channels on the muscle membrane, eliciting an action potential that causes contraction. Step 2: if the sodium channels cannot open, ACh is released but no action potential is produced in the muscle cell, so contraction cannot follow.","w":["Her nerves still release neurotransmitter normally.","The block is at the sodium channels, before contraction.","No action potential forms if the channels cannot open; satellite cells are for repair.","ACh opens sodium channels on the muscle membrane."],"k":"NMJ: ACh → Na⁺ channels → muscle AP → contraction.","rn":[["","NMJ: first 2 steps?","NMJ: first 2 steps?","1. ACh released; 2. Sodium channels open"]]},{"id":11125,"t":"Classification of Skeletal Muscle","s":"A physiotherapist watches a 20-year-old athlete kick a ball. As the knee straightens, the quadriceps contract strongly and the hamstrings relax. Then, as the athlete bends the knee to run, the hamstrings contract and the quadriceps relax. Meanwhile, muscles around the hip contract isometrically to keep it steady without moving it.<br><br>Which statement correctly describes the muscle roles?","o":["Roles switch: quadriceps are agonist in extension, antagonist in flexion; hip muscles are fixators","The quadriceps are always the agonist at the knee, whatever the movement","The hamstrings are synergists in both movements, as they prevent all unwanted knee movement","The hip muscles are agonists because they contract","The quadriceps are fixators in extension; the hamstrings are agonists in both"],"e":"Step 1: the agonist is the prime mover and the antagonist opposes it and must relax first; the quadriceps are the agonist extending the knee, and when the hamstrings (biceps femoris) flex the knee, the quadriceps are the antagonist. Step 2: roles are not fixed, and fixators contract isometrically, increasing tone without movement to stabilise a joint, as the hip muscles do here.","w":["Roles are not fixed; the quadriceps are antagonists in knee flexion.","The hamstrings are the antagonist in extension and agonist in flexion.","The hip muscles produce no movement, so they are fixators.","The quadriceps produce extension, so they are the agonist there."],"k":"Agonist/antagonist roles swap; fixators contract isometrically.","rn":[["","What is an antagonist?","What is an antagonist?","Opposes the agonist; Relaxes before agonist contracts"],["","What are the 4 Functional Classes of muscle?","What are the 4 Functional Classes of muscle?","Agonist; Antagonist; Synergist; Fixator"],["","Example of an antagonist","Example of an antagonist","Quadriceps femoris; When biceps femoris flexes knee"]]},{"id":11126,"t":"Repair and Regeneration of Muscle","s":"A 22-year-old starts weight training. After six months, his biceps are larger and stronger, with a greater cross-sectional area. A biopsy shows that the number of muscle fibres has not changed, but each fibre is bigger. Cells under the basement membrane have divided and fused with existing fibres.<br><br>What process has occurred, and which cells contributed?","o":["Hypertrophy; satellite cells fusing with existing fibres","Hyperplasia; satellite cells forming many new fibres","Atrophy; myoblasts breaking down fibres","Hyperplasia; cardiac stem cells moving into skeletal muscle","Hypertrophy; fibrocytes in the tendons laying down new collagen in the muscle"],"e":"Step 1: hypertrophy is an increase in cell size, giving increased muscle size, mass and cross-sectional area, usually through exercise; hyperplasia (more cells) probably does not occur normally. Step 2: satellite cells between the sarcolemma and basement membrane divide and fuse with existing fibres to lay down new proteins, causing hypertrophy.","w":["The number of fibres has not changed, so it is not hyperplasia.","Atrophy is wasting, not growth.","Cardiac muscle has no stem cells, and fibre number has not changed.","Fibrocytes are inactive tendon cells, not the cause of hypertrophy."],"k":"Exercise → hypertrophy via satellite cell fusion.","rn":[["","How do satellite cells help repair a muscle fibre?","How do satellite cells help repair a muscle fibre?","Fuse to the existing fibre; Donate their nuclei; Lay down new proteins"],["","What is hypertrophy?","What is hypertrophy?","Increase in cell size; Usually through exercise"]]},{"id":11127,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"A sports scientist compares two muscles of similar size. Muscle X has its tendon running down the centre with fibres attached obliquely on both sides, like a feather. Muscle Y is fusiform, with fibres running parallel along its length. Muscle X produces more force.<br><br>What type is muscle X, and why is it stronger?","o":["Bipennate; more fibres give a larger cross-sectional area","Unipennate; the tendon is along one side only","Bipennate; its fibres are longer, so they shorten more","Multipennate; it is made of smooth muscle","Fusiform; force is not related to cross-sectional area but to how long each muscle fibre is"],"e":"Step 1: a central tendon with fibres on both sides is bipennate (e.g. rectus femoris), whereas unipennate muscles have the tendon along one side. Step 2: skeletal muscle force is proportional to cross-sectional area, and pennate muscles pack in more fibres, giving a larger cross-sectional area and more force.","w":["Muscle X has fibres on both sides, so it is bipennate.","Its strength comes from cross-sectional area, not longer fibres.","It is skeletal muscle, and it is bipennate.","Muscle Y is fusiform, and force is proportional to cross-sectional area."],"k":"Pennate muscles: more fibres → bigger cross-section → more force.","rn":[["","What is the force of skeletal muscle proportional to?","What is the force of skeletal muscle proportional to?","Cross-sectional area"],["","Why are pennate muscles stronger than parallel-fibre muscles?","Why are pennate muscles stronger than parallel-fibre muscles?","Bigger cross-sectional area"]]},{"id":11128,"t":"Components of Muscle Tissue","s":"A surgeon repairing a torn thigh muscle describes the layers she sees. She first cuts through a sheath around the whole muscle, then separates bundles of fibres each wrapped in their own sheath. Under a microscope, a pathologist then sees a fine layer around each individual muscle cell.<br><br>Which sequence of layers, from outside in, is correct?","o":["Epimysium, perimysium, endomysium","Endomysium, perimysium, epimysium","Perimysium, epimysium, endomysium","Epimysium, fascicle, sarcolemma","Perimysium, endomysium, epimysium, with the fascicle as the innermost wrapping"],"e":"Step 1: the epimysium surrounds the entire muscle (outermost), the perimysium surrounds each bundle (fascicle) and the endomysium surrounds each individual fibre (innermost). Step 2: a fascicle is the bundle itself, not a wrapping, so the order from outside in is epimysium, perimysium, endomysium.","w":["This is the order from inside out.","The epimysium is outermost.","A fascicle is a bundle, not a wrapping, and the sarcolemma is the cell membrane.","The fascicle is a bundle, and the epimysium is outermost."],"k":"Epimysium (muscle) → perimysium (fascicle) → endomysium (fibre).","rn":[["","What are the 3 connective tissue layers of a muscle?","What are the 3 connective tissue layers of a muscle?","Epimysium; Perimysium; Endomysium; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":11129,"t":"Repair and Regeneration of Muscle","s":"A 70-year-old woman is in bed for six weeks after a hip operation. She is also eating poorly and has poor circulation in her legs. When she starts walking again, her thigh muscles are visibly thinner and weaker. Her physiotherapist explains the change.<br><br>What has happened, and which of her factors are listed causes?","o":["Atrophy; lack of exercise, poor nutrition and poor circulation","Hypertrophy; lack of exercise and poor nutrition","Hyperplasia; poor circulation only","Atrophy; caused only by genetic factors, not by inactivity, diet or the circulation to the legs","Fibrosis; replacement of muscle by collagenous scar"],"e":"Step 1: atrophy is wasting of muscle, with thinner, weaker muscles. Step 2: listed causes of atrophy are lack of exercise, poor nutrition, poor circulation, neurological and genetic causes, and she has three of these.","w":["Hypertrophy is an increase in cell size.","Hyperplasia is an increase in cell number, probably only in tumours.","Inactivity, nutrition and circulation are listed causes.","Scarring is how cardiac muscle responds to death, not wasting from disuse."],"k":"Atrophy causes: inactivity, nutrition, circulation, neurological, genetic.","rn":[["","What are 3 lifestyle causes of atrophy?","What are 3 lifestyle causes of atrophy?","Lack of exercise; Poor nutrition; Poor circulation"]]},{"id":11130,"t":"Types of Muscle","s":"A pharmacology student studies a muscle from the wall of a blood vessel. It lacks stripes, contracts slowly and can hold a contraction for a long time. When its nerve supply is stimulated, the muscle responds, but the subject cannot control it voluntarily.<br><br>Which muscle type is this, and which nerves supply it?","o":["Smooth muscle; autonomic secretomotor nerves","Skeletal muscle; somatic and branchial motor nerves","Cardiac muscle; the vagus and sympathetic trunk only","Smooth muscle; somatic motor nerves acting at a neuromuscular junction","Skeletal muscle; visceral motor nerves, as in the walls of the heart and great vessels"],"e":"Step 1: smooth muscle has no stripes, elongated spindle-shaped fibres and slow, sustained contractions, and is found in blood vessel walls (tunica media). Step 2: smooth muscle is involuntary and innervated by autonomic secretomotor nerves.","w":["Skeletal muscle is striated and voluntary.","Cardiac muscle is striated and found in the heart and great vessels.","Somatic motor nerves supply skeletal muscle.","Skeletal muscle is striated and voluntary."],"k":"Smooth muscle: non-striated, slow sustained, autonomic secretomotor.","rn":[["","What nerves innervate Smooth Muscle?","What nerves innervate Smooth Muscle?","Autonomic secretomotor nerves"]]}]);
